{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.yorku.ca/unit/vpf/wp-content/uploads/sites/1070/2026/10/LIB_CLA_IAF.Up_.pdf": {
      "title": "applicants for an Adjunct Archivist position, Irving Abella Fonds. This is a Contractually Limited",
      "description": " \nAdjunct Archivist, Irving Abella Fonds  \nClara Thomas Archives & Special Collections, York University Libraries \n \nThe Clara Thomas Archives & Special Collections at York University Libraries invites highly qualified \napplicants for an Adjunct Archivist position, Irving Abella Fonds. This is a Contractually Limited \nAppointment (CLA) for a one-year term to commence January 1, 2027. \n \nThis position is focused on the arrangement and description of the substantial archives of Dr. Irving \nAbella (1940-2022), professor emeritus of York University, historian, and important contributor to the \ndevelopment of Canadian Jewish Studies as a discipline of study. The Clara Thomas Archives & \nSpecial Collections is a small but dynamic department of York University Libraries and is located in the \nScott Library on the Keele campus. Its archival holdings consist of more than eight kilometres of \nuniversity records and private papers, including an extensive collection of non-textual materials. \nSpecial Collections has over 128,000 items of published Canadiana and other works. Additional \ninformation on holdings and services can be found at:  http://www.library.yorku.ca/web/archives. \n  \nProjects and Responsibilities: \nThe Adjunct Archivist works within a collaborative team environment with work coordinated by the \nHead of the archives. The incumbent will be responsible for the appraisal, arrangement, description, \nand physical processing of the archival records of Irving Abella onsite at the Scott Library, Keele \nCampus. In addition, the incumbent, as a member of the archives team, will contribute to \ndepartmental operations, including:  \n• provision of archival reference services in an academic setting; \n• instruction in archival literacy; \n• knowledge mobilization in relation to the records of Irving Abella; including the creation of \nmetadata of any relevant material digitized for deposit in the university’s digital heritage \npreservation repository, York University Digital Library (YUDL); \n• service including participation in the work of the Libraries’ committees as appropriate; and \n• pursuit of scholarship, professional development, and service. \n \nWhile the bulk of the work will be the arrangement and description of the papers of Irving Abella, \nother small processing projects may be assigned in alignment with any demonstrated areas of \nlinguistic or disciplinary strength and expertise. \n \nCandidate Qualifications: \n• Degree: Master’s degree in archival studies from an ALA-accredited program or Master of Library \nand Information Studies (or equivalent) with concentration in professional archival education \nfrom an ALA-accredited program, by the appointment’s start is required. \n• Required Experience: \no At least one year of professional experience in an archives; \no Excellent interpersonal, communication, and presentation skills; \no Ability to undertake physical processing and handling of records; \no Ability to work independently and in collaboration with others; \no Superior written and spoken communication in English; \n\n \no Evidence or promise of research contributions in the form of publications, \npresentations, or other scholarly output, professional development, and active \ninvolvement in professional or scholarly organizations; \n• Demonstrated: \no Expertise in processing and providing access to archival records within an automated \nwork environment, including working knowledge of the Rules for Archival Description; \nexperience with Access to Memory AtoM would be an asset. \no Project management skills, including the ability to manage detail-oriented workflow \nwith a high degree of accuracy within established deadlines.  \no Experience with working in established archives, preferably in an academic setting. \no Experience with teaching, presenting, and providing tracking and/or instruction. \n• Knowledge of: \no Copyright and privacy legislation and its application to archival holdings and reference \nservices; \no Standards and best practices for the preservation and use of analogue and digital \narchival records; and  \no Workflow associated with digitization and curation of digital objects. \n• Additional: Familiarity with the history of Jewish culture, traditions, and historical narratives, \nparticularly in relation to the Jewish diaspora in Canada, is an asset. \n \nHiring Policies: \n•  The anticipated starting salary for this position at the rank of Adjunct Archivist is in the range of \n$85,000 to $115,000 and will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. York \nUniversity offers generous benefits and salary provisions. All York University positions are subject \nto budgetary approval. \n• York is an Affirmative Action (AA) employer and strongly values diversity, including gender and \nsexual diversity, in its community. Details of the AA Program, which applies to women, members \nof racialized groups, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities and those who identify as \n2SLGBTQ+, can be found here or by contacting Aqsa Ahmed, EDI Program Manager \n(aqsa517@yorku.ca).  \n• York welcomes and employs scholars from all over the world. All qualified candidates are \nencouraged to apply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority.  \n• York has a policy on Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities and is \ncommitted to working towards a barrier-free workplace and expanding the accessibility of the \nworkplace to persons with disabilities. Applicants who require accommodation are invited to \ncontact the Search Committee Chair, Anna St. Onge, yulapps@yorku.ca. \n \nApplication Process: \n• Due date for completed applications: November 2, 2026. Interviews will be conducted in-person, \nscheduled in mid-November 2026 with an interview in the morning and additional meetings in the \nafternoon.  Please contact the chair of the search committee should any accommodations be \nrequired. \n• Required materials: 1) current CV and a cover letter addressing the stated qualifications and \nexperience relevant to the responsibilities; 3) Names and contact information for three \n \nprofessional references. References will only be contacted for candidates who proceed to a final \ninterview and will be due no later than November 20, 2026. \n• Provide required information regarding your Canadian work status and optional self-identification \nfor Affirmative Action purposes at this link: YUWorkAA. \n• Direct questions about the position can be sent to the Search Committee Chair, Anna St. Onge at \nyulapps@yorku.ca. \n• Submit materials to: yulapps@yorku.ca  \n \nLearn More About York: \n• York University generates and shares knowledge through our research, teaching and engagement \nwith communities around the world. The 2023-2028 Strategic Research Plan showcases the \ndepth, breadth and ambition of research at York. \n• York’s commitments to social justice are laid out in our Decolonizing, Equity, Diversity and \nInclusion Strategy, the Framework & Action Plan on Black Inclusion and the Indigenous \nFramework for York University. \n• Follow the activities and accomplishments of York’s faculty, students and staff on YFile. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nFirst Nations peoples have lived on this part of Turtle Island for millennia, stewarding the land, the water and all that con tributes to life in this \nregion. Today, the culture and presence of First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples enrich the lands and people of this territory. More than two \ncenturies ago, the Mississauga people welcomed settlers to this territory, providing sustenance and engaging in trade and com merce. Today, York \nUniversity campuses are located on the Toronto Purchase Treaty No. 13 lands and territory of the Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation and the \nlands of the Anishinabek of the Williams Treaties First Nations. Our campuses are also situated on the traditional territorie s of the Huron-Wendat \nand the Haudenosaunee. Treaty history is foundational, and it is our collective responsibility to honour the land, as we honour and respect those \nwho have gone before us, those who are here and those who have yet to come. We are grateful for the opportunity to be learnin g, working and \nthriving on this land, and we commit to learn the truth and be active in the process of reconciliation. "
    },
    "https://www.yorku.ca/unit/vpf/wp-content/uploads/sites/1070/2026/10/HH_KINE_PhyCul.pdf": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Critical Studies of Physical Culture and Health",
      "description": " \nAssistant Professor in Critical Studies of Physical Culture and Health \nSchool of Kinesiology and Health Science, Faculty of Health \n \nThe School of Kinesiology and Health Science and the Faculty of Health at York University invite highly qualified \napplicants for an Assistant Professor position in Critical Studies of Physical Culture and Health. This is an open \ntenure-track appointment to the Professorial Stream to commence July 1, 2027. \n \nPhysical culture (including sport, movement, physical education, and fitness) and health are deeply shaped by \nbroader social, political, and institutional forces, locally and globally. These influences demand sustained critical \nscholarly attention to how power, inequality, and institutional structures shape embodied practices, health \noutcomes, and health inequities for communities worldwide. The successful candidate will bring expertise in the \ncritical study of physical culture and health, examining how social, political, and institutional forces shape \nbodies, institutions, and health inequities, specifically in research areas related to militarization, nationalism, \nwarfare, security and surveillance regimes, and border enforcement. This position advances policy-relevant \nresearch that supports just, peaceful, and inclusive societies, in keeping with York’s commitment to the United \nNations’ Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs)-focused scholarship, including SDG-16 (Peace, Justice and \nStrong Institutions), and bridges health sciences with critical social theory, political economy, and justice-\noriented inquiry. \n \nThis appointment builds on existing strengths within the School of Kinesiology and Health Science, positioning \nthe School to lead internationally in examining how sport, physical culture, and health are embedded within \nbroader regimes of social, political, and institutional forces. The successful candidate will strengthen the \nSchool’s leadership in examining how physical culture and health are shaped by broader social, political, and \ninstitutional forces; and extend the School’s curricular priorities, by contributing to undergraduate and graduate \ncourses that critically examine how power, violence, and technology shape embodied experiences, and will \nsupport new programming that includes the Undergraduate Certificate in Social Justice and Physical Activity and \nthe development of a Professional Master’s in Policy Studies in Sport and Physical Activity. \n \nCandidate Qualifications: \n• Degree: PhD in Kinesiology, Health Studies, Sociology, Equity Studies, or a related field, is required.  \n• Scholarship: \no A coherent and well-articulated program of research and specialization examining the critical study of \nphysical culture and health as it pertains to the detailed position description above. \no A record or evident promise of generating innovative, substantive, rigorous, and as appropriate, externally \nfunded research.  \no A record or evident promise of making influential contributions and demonstrating excellence to the \ncritical study of physical culture and health as it pertains to the detailed description above (e.g., \npublications, conference presentations, awards, policy contributions, curriculum, knowledge \nmobilization). \no A record or evident promise of engaging in community-based research or partnerships in the critical study \nof physical culture and health as it pertains to the description above, is considered an asset. \n• Teaching: \no A record or evident promise of excellence in teaching and dedication to students (e.g., teaching \naccomplishments, pedagogical innovations, student and peer evaluations, awards).  \no Suitability for prompt appointment to the Faculty of Graduate Studies, given that the position will involve \ngraduate teaching and supervision. \no Willingness and ability to teach and contribute to the School’s undergraduate and graduate programs, \nincluding the Undergraduate Certificate in Social Justice and Physical Activity and the Professional \nMaster’s in Policy Studies in Sport and Physical Activity. \n\n \n \nHiring Policies: \n• The anticipated starting salary for this position at the rank of Assistant Professor is in the range of $132,000 \nto $162,000 and will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. York University offers generous \nbenefits and salary provisions. \n• All York University positions are subject to budgetary approval. \n• York is an Affirmative Action (AA) employer and strongly values diversity, including gender and sexual \ndiversity, in its community. Details of the AA Program, which applies to women, members of racialized \ngroups, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities and those who identify as 2SLGBTQ+, can be found \nhere or by contacting Aqsa Ahmed, EDI Program Manager (aqsa517@yorku.ca).  \n• York welcomes and employs scholars from all over the world. All qualified candidates are encouraged to \napply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority.  \n• York has a policy on Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities and is committed to \nworking towards a barrier-free workplace and expanding the accessibility of the workplace to persons with \ndisabilities. Applicants who require accommodation are invited to contact Dr. Nicolette Richardson, Chair, \nSchool of Kinesiology and Health Science,  kinchair@yorku.ca. \n \nApplication Process: \n• Due date for completed applications: November 20, 2026 \n• Required materials: 1) current CV; 2) cover letter; 3) one-page program of research statement; 4) one-page \nteaching statement, with supporting materials; and 5) names and contact information for three references. \nReference letters will only be requested from shortlisted candidates. \n• Provide required information regarding your Canadian work status and optional self-identification for \nAffirmative Action purposes at this link: YUWorkAA.  \n• Direct questions about the position to Dr. Nicolette Richardson, Chair, School of Kinesiology and Health \nScience, kinchair@yorku.ca. \n• Submit materials to: kinchair@yorku.ca. \n \nLearn More About York: \n• York University generates and shares knowledge through our research, teaching and engagement with \ncommunities around the world. The 2023-2028 Strategic Research Plan showcases the depth, breadth and \nambition of research at York. \n• York’s commitments to social justice are laid out in our Decolonizing, Equity, Diversity and Inclusion \nStrategy, the Framework & Action Plan on Black Inclusion and the Indigenous Framework for York \nUniversity. \n• Follow the activities and accomplishments of York’s faculty, students and staff on YFile. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nFirst Nations peoples have lived on this part of Turtle Island for millennia, stewarding the land, the water and all that con tributes to life in this \nregion. Today, the culture and presence of First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples enrich the lands and people of this territory. More than two \ncenturies ago, the Mississauga people welcomed settlers to this territory, providing sustenance and engaging in trade and com merce. Today, York \nUniversity campuses are located on the Toronto Purchase Treaty No. 13 lands and territory of the Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation and the \nlands of the Anishinabek of the Williams Treaties First Nations. Our campuses are also situated on the traditional territorie s of the Huron-Wendat \nand the Haudenosaunee. Treaty history is foundational, and it is our collective responsibility to honour the land, as we honour and respect those \nwho have gone before us, those who are here and those who have yet to come. We are grateful for the opportunity to be learnin g, working and \nthriving on this land, and we commit to learn the truth and be active in the process of reconciliation. "
    },
    "https://www.yorku.ca/unit/vpf/wp-content/uploads/sites/1070/2026/09/AMPD_FMA_Prod.pdf": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Film Production Generalist, with a focus on alternative/experimental filmmaking",
      "description": " \nAssistant Professor in Film Production Generalist, with a focus on alternative/experimental filmmaking \nDepartment of Film & Media Arts, School of the Arts, Media, Performance and Design \n \nThe Department of Film & Media Arts in the School of the Arts, Media, Performance and Design \n(AMPD) at York University invites highly qualified applicants for an Assistant Professor position in Film \nProduction - Generalist, with a focus on alternative/experimental filmmaking. This is an open tenure-\ntrack appointment to the Professorial Stream to commence July 1, 2027. \n \nThe successful candidate will teach introductory and advanced undergraduate and graduate courses \nin alternative/experimental film and media processes, including foundational aspects of the film \nproduction and post-production curriculum (e.g. camera, picture editing, and sound), and will \nsupervise graduate students. Teaching may include, but is not limited to, filmmaking, writing, \ndirecting, producing, editing, and/or distribution across multiple film and media practices. The \nsuccessful candidate will also be expected to engage energetically and collegially in Department \ngovernance and in administering the program, and to generate national and international academic, \nfilm community, and/or industry partnerships. \n \nThe Department of Film and Media Arts is Canada’s first and foremost film school, with four \nspecialized streams of undergraduate study — Film Production, Film Studies and Creative Media \nIndustries, Media Arts, and Screenwriting and Creative Producing— alongside MA, MFA, and PhD \ngraduate degrees, all taught in excellent production facilities. The Department and its Graduate \nProgram have strong connections to industry as well as to independent film and media arts \ncommunity organizations, and a stellar faculty of internationally renowned filmmakers, screenwriters, \nmedia artists, and scholars. \n \nYork is a leading international teaching and research university, and a driving force for positive change. \nEmpowered by a welcoming and diverse community with a uniquely global perspective, we are \npreparing our students for their long-term careers and personal success. Together, we can make \npositive change for our communities, our planet, and our future. \n \nWe acknowledge that within higher education in Canada, traditional or conventional academic \npathways in research can reinforce biases in the filling of faculty posts. We encourage applications \nthat may not fit all of the qualifications below and that challenge our ideas of teaching, scholarship, \nand creative research/production. We are particularly interested in colleagues who bring experience in \nfacilitating equity, diversity, and inclusion practices within their teaching, which may include contexts \noutside of academia such as artistic praxis, collaborative, and community-based work. \n \nCandidate Qualifications: \n• Degree: PhD or PhD equivalent such as an MFA/MA with a substantial record of professional \nexperience, with a focus on creative activity in film and/or digital production or a closely related \nprofessional field, by the appointment’s start is required.  \n• Creative Practice/Scholarship: \no A coherent and well-articulated program of research creation and/or professional practice and \nspecialization in alternative/experimental filmmaking and related film and media production. \n\n \no A record or evident promise of generative innovative, substantive, rigorous, and as appropriate, \nexternally funded research and/or creative practice, as demonstrated in the submitted \nstatement of professional practice and CV. \no A record or evident promise of making influential contributions and demonstrating excellence in \nthe field (e.g., a record of analog, digital, and/or transmedia production, screenings at major film \nand media festivals, and related activities and accolades within the Canadian and international \narts and film communities). \no Knowledge of analog workflow (e.g., the capture, development, and printing of film), as well as \nof digital asset management workflow. \no Strong knowledge of trends and practices in experimental and new media, and knowledge of \ndiverse cultural contexts. \n• Teaching: \no A record or evident promise of excellence in teaching and dedication to students, including \nprofessional experience mentoring students through the stages of project development (e.g., \nteaching evaluations, masterclasses, specialized filmmaking workshops, mentorship, examples \nof collaborative practice, and other teaching accomplishments). \no A record or evident promise to bring both theoretical and practical orientation to teaching, \nlearning, and program design in the area of alternative/experimental filmmaking, and to teach \ngeneral introductory undergraduate and advanced graduate film production courses to students \nengaged in fiction, documentary, alternative/experimental, and other media arts practices. \no A record or evident promise of engaging in curriculum design and development according to \npedagogical needs. \no A commitment to pedagogical innovation, including in high-priority areas such as experiential \neducation, technology-enhanced learning, and multi-platform approaches. \no Suitability for prompt appointment to the Faculty of Graduate Studies, given that the position will \ninvolve graduate teaching and supervision. \n• Desirable knowledge and/or experience: Practical knowledge of emergent technologies, such as \nthe application of A.I. in the creative process. \no Experience in other modes of filmmaking, including on-set experience and collaboration in \nfiction and documentary films and in future-oriented media. \no Knowledge of the Canadian Film Industry. Ability to navigate global festival markets, and festival \npartnerships, plus knowledge of domestic and international festival programs and/or \ndistribution, and an understanding of how Toronto relates to the global film culture that would \nbe relevant to our emerging and diverse student body. \no Knowledge of world cinemas and the latest production themes and approaches. \no Experience of working with a variety of funding models and modes of production. \no Ability to collaborate with peer faculty across various genres and specialties. \no Skills in production management, programming, and/or distribution. \no A record of professional experience collaborating with women, diverse sexual identities, and \nIBPOC (Indigenous, Black, People of Colour) artists and communities. \n \nHiring Policies: \n• The anticipated starting salary for this position at the rank of Assistant Professor is in the range of \n$130,000 to $160,000 and will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. York \nUniversity offers generous benefits and salary provisions. \n \n• All York University positions are subject to budgetary approval. \n• York is an Affirmative Action (AA) employer and strongly values diversity, including gender and \nsexual diversity, in its community. Details of the AA Program, which applies to women, members \nof racialized groups, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities and those who identify as \n2SLGBTQ+, can be found here or by contacting Aqsa Ahmed, EDI Program Manager \n(aqsa517@yorku.ca).  \n• York welcomes and employs scholars from all over the world. All qualified candidates are \nencouraged to apply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority.  \n• York has a policy on Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities and is \ncommitted to working towards a barrier-free workplace and expanding the accessibility of the \nworkplace to persons with disabilities. Applicants who require accommodation are invited to \ncontact Jennifer Malisani, Administrative Assistant, Department of Cinema & Media Arts, at \nfmsearch@yorku.ca. \n \nApplication Process: \n• Due date for completed applications: November 18, 2026. \n• Required materials: 1) cover letter; 2) current curriculum vitae (CV); 3) teaching statement and \ndossier (including teaching evaluations, where applicable); 4) statement of professional practice; \n5) links to film/media productions; and 6) the names and contact information of three referees \nwho can provide letters of reference. Only short-listed candidates’ references will be contacted. \n• Provide required information regarding your Canadian work status and optional self-identification \nfor Affirmative Action at this link: YUWorkAA. \n• Direct questions about the position to Caitlin Fisher, Chair, Department of Cinema & Media Arts, at \nfmsearch@yorku.ca. \n• Submit materials: to fmsearch@yorku.ca, as a single PDF no larger than 20 MB.  \n \nLearn More About York: \n• York University generates and shares knowledge through our research, teaching and engagement \nwith communities around the world. The 2023-2028 Strategic Research Plan showcases the \ndepth, breadth and ambition of research at York. \n• York’s commitments to social justice are laid out in our Decolonizing, Equity, Diversity and \nInclusion Strategy, the Framework & Action Plan on Black Inclusion and the Indigenous \nFramework for York University. \n• Follow the activities and accomplishments of York’s faculty, students and staff on YFile. \n \n \n \n \n \nFirst Nations peoples have lived on this part of Turtle Island for millennia, stewarding the land, the water and all that con tributes to life in this region. \nToday, the culture and presence of First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples enrich the lands and people of this territory. More than two centuries ago, \nthe Mississauga people welcomed settlers to this territory, providing sustenance and engaging in trade and commerce. Today, Y ork University \ncampuses are located on the Toronto Purchase Treaty No. 13 lands and territory of the Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation and the lands of the \nAnishinabek of the Williams Treaties First Nations. Our campuses are also situated on the traditional territories of the Huro n-Wendat and the \nHaudenosaunee. Treaty history is foundational, and it is our collective responsibility to honour the land, as we honour and respect those who have \ngone before us, those who are here and those who have yet to come. We are grateful for the opportunity to be learning, workin g and thriving on this \nland, and we commit to learn the truth and be active in the process of reconciliation. "
    },
    "https://www.yorku.ca/unit/vpf/wp-content/uploads/sites/1070/2026/09/AMPD_MUS_Tech.pdf": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Music Technology and Production",
      "description": " \nAssistant Professor in Music Technology and Production  \nDepartment of Music, School of the Arts, Media, Performance and Design \n \nThe Department of Music in the School of the Arts, Media, Performance and Design at York University \ninvites highly qualified applicants for an Assistant Professor position in Music Technology and \nProduction. This is an open tenure-track appointment to the Professorial Stream to commence July 1, \n2027. \n \nWe seek an imaginative and innovative scholar-practitioner who will provide creative research and \neducational leadership to enhance and expand the existing music curriculum through the further \ndevelopment of live sound, recording engineering, mixing, mastering, and immersive sound (including \nDolby Atmos), and music production. The successful candidate will have opportunities to teach both \nundergraduate and graduate courses and to supervise graduate students. The candidate will be \nexpected to submit an application for a Social Sciences and Humanities Research Council (SSHRC) \ngrant within the first two to three years of employment.  \n \nThe successful candidate will demonstrate the capacity to bring a theoretical and practical orientation \nto recording engineering and music production, model the creative use of audio technology in music \nproduction and performance, and contribute to capstone projects and industry connections. Because \nthis is a highly collaborative program, we are looking for a specialist who can also contribute to \nexisting curricular areas of the program, preferably with additional expertise in one or more of the \nfollowing areas: classical music, jazz, popular music, songwriting/composition for media, or world \nmusic. \n \nWe acknowledge that within higher education in Canada, traditional or conventional academic and \nartistic pathways can reinforce biases in the filling of faculty posts. We encourage applications that \nmay not fit all of the qualifications and that challenge our ideas of teaching, scholarship, research and \nartistic practice. We are particularly interested in colleagues who bring experience in facilitating \nequity, diversity and inclusion practices within their teaching and research, including in contexts \noutside academia, such as artistic praxis and community-based work. \n \nCandidate Qualifications: \n• Degree: PhD or DMA or a PhD or DMA near completion by the appointment’s start; or a PhD-\nequivalent specialized doctorate, such as a DMus or EdD; or an MA or MFA combined with a \nsubstantial record of nationally or internationally recognized professional experience, \nresearch/creation, publications, or equivalent creative or professional achievement in a relevant \nfield is required. \n• Scholarship: \no A coherent and well-articulated program of research/research-creation and/or professional \npractice and specialization focused on audio technology. \no A record or evident promise of generating innovative, substantive, rigorous, and as appropriate, \nexternally funded research and/or research-creation. \n\n \no A record or evident promise of making influential contributions and demonstrating excellence in \nthe field (e.g., a research statement outlining a program of scholarly activity and a coherent \nresearch trajectory).  \n• Teaching: \no A record or evident promise of excellence in teaching and dedication to students (e.g., a teaching \nstatement; teaching accomplishments and pedagogical innovations, including in high-priority \nareas such as experiential education, technology-enhanced learning and community-based \nlearning; teaching evaluations). \no Experience with curriculum development and universal design approaches to teaching and \nassessment is preferred. The successful candidate is also expected to provide evidence of \nservice contributions or the potential to contribute through administrative and committee work. \no Suitability for prompt appointment to the Faculty of Graduate Studies, given that the position will \ninvolve graduate teaching and supervision. \n• Additional: Experience in the production of a variety of musical styles including those currently \nrepresented in the department as well as others emerging from contemporary global and Black \nmusical expression is an asset. \no Familiarity with video production, visual media, or multimedia performance would be an asset \nbut is not required. \n \nHiring Policies: \n• The anticipated starting salary for this position at the rank of Assistant Professor is in the range of \n$130,000 to $160,000 and will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. York \nUniversity offers generous benefits and salary provisions.  \n• All York University positions are subject to budgetary approval. \n• York is an Affirmative Action (AA) employer and strongly values diversity, including gender and \nsexual diversity, in its community. Details of the AA Program, which applies to women, members of \nracialized groups, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities, and those who identify as \n2SLGBTQ+, can be found here or by contacting Aqsa Ahmed, EDI Program Manager \n(aqsa517@yorku.ca).  \n• York welcomes and employs scholars from all over the world. All qualified candidates are \nencouraged to apply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority.  \n• York has a policy on Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities and is \ncommitted to working towards a barrier-free workplace and expanding the accessibility of the \nworkplace to persons with disabilities. Applicants who require accommodation are invited to \ncontact Lora Zuech, Administrative Assistant of the Department of Music, zuechl@yorku.ca. \n \nApplication Process: \n• Due date for completed applications: November 18, 2026 \n• Required materials: 1) current CV; 2) signed letter of application outlining professional experience; \n3) research statement; 4) teaching dossier that supports excellence and innovation in teaching \n(this may include items such as sample course outlines, statement of teaching philosophy, \nworkshop and professional development programs attended or led, and conference papers, \npublications, recordings, or other creative works relating to research and pedagogy, or other \nrelevant materials); 5) names and contact information of three referees who can speak to your \n \nqualifications and experience in relation to the position. Letters of recommendation will be \nsolicited at a later date in the process from short-listed candidates only.  \n• Provide required information regarding your Canadian work status and optional self-identification \nfor Affirmative Action purposes at this link: YUWorkAA. \n• Direct questions about the position to Lora Zuech, Administrative Assistant of the Department of \nMusic, zuechl@yorku.ca. \n• Submit materials: to music-search@yorku.ca, as a single PDF no larger than 20 MB. \n \nLearn More About York: \n• York University generates and shares knowledge through our research, teaching and engagement \nwith communities around the world. The 2023-2028 Strategic Research Plan showcases the \ndepth, breadth and ambition of research at York. \n• York’s commitments to social justice are laid out in our Decolonizing, Equity, Diversity and \nInclusion Strategy, the Framework & Action Plan on Black Inclusion and the Indigenous \nFramework for York University. \n• Follow the activities and accomplishments of York’s faculty, students and staff on YFile. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nFirst Nations peoples have lived on this part of Turtle Island for millennia, stewarding the land, the water and all that con tributes to life in this region. \nToday, the culture and presence of First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples enrich the lands and people of this territory. More than two centuries ago, \nthe Mississauga people welcomed settlers to this territory, providing sustenance and engaging in trade and commerce. Today, Y ork University \ncampuses are located on the Toronto Purchase Treaty No. 13 lands and territory of the Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation and the lands of the \nAnishinabek of the Williams Treaties First Nations. Our campuses are also situated on the traditional territories of the Huro n-Wendat and the \nHaudenosaunee. Treaty history is foundational, and it is our collective responsibility to honour the land, as we honour and respect those who have \ngone before us, those who are here and those who have yet to come. We are grateful for the opportunity to be learning, workin g and thriving on this \nland, and we commit to learn the truth and be active in the process of reconciliation. "
    },
    "https://www.yorku.ca/unit/vpf/wp-content/uploads/sites/1070/2026/10/HH_GH_PH.pdf": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Public Health",
      "description": " \nAssistant Professor in Public Health \nSchool of Global Health, Faculty of Health \n \nThe School of Global Health in the Faculty of Health at York University invites highly qualified \napplicants for an Assistant Professor position in Public Health. This is an open tenure-track \nappointment to the Professorial Stream to commence July 1, 2027. \n \nThe School of Global Health consists of a highly committed interdisciplinary group of scholars and \neducators with a diverse focus across healthcare, public health, social determinants of health, and \nplanetary health. It offers the first dedicated undergraduate global health degree program in Canada \nand a highly distinctive interdisciplinary PhD program in global health. Through cross-disciplinary \nprogramming, innovative course design, diverse experiential learning, and a dynamic and supportive \nacademic community environment, our students receive the training and education to create big ideas \nthat make an impact on the world. \n \nWe invite applications from candidates whose scholarship addresses complex challenges in global \nand public health through innovative, community-engaged, and equity-oriented approaches. We \nespecially welcome applications from candidates whose work contributes to transforming the \nstructures, systems, and knowledge practices that produce health inequities. Relevant research \nspecializations in global and public health may include planetary health and climate justice; \nimplementation science and evaluation; transdisciplinary approaches; artificial intelligence and data \njustice; decolonizing approaches and Indigenous health sovereignty; syndemics and complex \nchallenges; health economics; clinical epidemiology; and other relevant specializations advancing \nglobal and public health equity. \n \nThe successful candidate will demonstrate the ability to connect global health research with public \nhealth policy and practice and to work reciprocally with communities, Indigenous partners, public \nhealth agencies, governments, and other organizations in Canada and internationally. Importantly, the \nsuccessful candidate will contribute to the development, delivery, student supervision, and evaluation \nof an accredited professional Master of Public Health (MPH) with a concentration in Global Health that \nprepares graduates for leadership in public and global health practice, policy, programs, and research. \nThey will also support the overall growth, collegial governance processes, and academic community \nengagement activities covering the School’s undergraduate and doctoral programs.     \n \nCandidate Qualifications: \n• Degree: Master of Public Health (MPH); and PhD or ScD by the appointment’s start in global \nhealth, public health, health services or systems research, epidemiology, biostatistics, health \npolicy, implementation science, social and behavioural sciences, environmental health, \nIndigenous health, or a related field, are required. \n• Scholarship: \no A coherent and well-articulated program of research and specialization in the fields of global \nhealth and public health. \n\n \no A record or evident promise of effective engagement with public health policy and/or program \nstakeholders (e.g., government, public health agencies, health care organizations, community \norganizations) in international settings in the context of research or professional practice. \no A record or evident promise of generating innovative, substantive, rigorous, and as appropriate, \nexternally funded research in global health and public health. \no A record or evident promise of making diverse and influential scholarly or professional \ncontributions and demonstrating excellence in the fields of global health and public health (e.g., \npublications in key journals, major presentations, community contributions, policy impacts, \nawards and/or accolades). \n• Teaching: \no A record or evident promise of teaching one or more core public health subjects and on \nfundamental global health principles and practices. \no A record or evident promise of excellence in teaching and dedication to student success and \nprofessional skills development (e.g., teaching accomplishments, pedagogical innovations, \nstudent and peer evaluations, awards). \no A record or evident promise of interdisciplinary, experiential, learner-engaged, and decolonial \nteaching, mentorship, and supervision.  \no Suitable for prompt appointment to the Faculty of Graduate Studies, given that the position will \ninvolve graduate teaching and supervision. \nAdditional:  \no Completion of a Council on Education for Public Health (CEPH) accredited MPH program or a \nrecord or evident promise of knowledge of CEPH accredited MPH programs. \no A record or evident promise of teaching in a public health or global health program is preferred. \no A record or evident promise of developing, implementing, or enhancing graduate-level curricula \nis preferred. \no A record or evident promise of establishing and sustaining formal community partnerships in \nCanada or internationally is preferred. \no A record or evident promise of working in low- and middle-income countries, or with Indigenous \ncommunities, rural or remote communities, or historically underserved populations is preferred. \no A record or evident promise of coordinating or supervising student placements or related applied \nprojects in Canada or internationally is preferred. \n \nHiring Policies: \n• The anticipated starting salary for this position at the rank of Assistant Professor is in the range of \n$132,000 to $162,000 and will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. York \nUniversity offers generous benefits and salary provisions. \n• All York University positions are subject to budgetary approval. \n• York is an Affirmative Action (AA) employer and strongly values diversity, including gender and \nsexual diversity, in its community. Details of the AA Program, which applies to women, members \nof racialized groups, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities and those who identify as \n2SLGBTQ+, can be found here or by contacting Aqsa Ahmed, EDI Program Manager \n(aqsa517@yorku.ca). \n• York welcomes and employs scholars from all over the world. All qualified candidates are \nencouraged to apply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority. \n \n• York has a policy on Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities and is \ncommitted to working towards a barrier-free workplace and expanding the accessibility of the \nworkplace to persons with disabilities. Applicants who require accommodation are invited to \ncontact Amrita Daftary, Director, School of Global Health, gh_dir@yorku.ca \n \nApplication Process: \n• Due date for completed applications: November 20, 2026 \n• Required materials: 1) cover letter, specifying interest and suitability for the position (maximum 2 \npages); 2) current CV, including all academic, scholarly, professional, teaching and related \nqualifications and achievements that are relevant to the position (maximum 12 pages); 3) research \nstatement (maximum 2 pages); 4) up to 3 scholarly or professional practice outputs relevant to \nthis position (specify individual contribution if this is not evident within the outputs); 5) teaching \ndossier (maximum 12 pages); and 6) the names and contact information of 3 referees willing to \nprovide a confidential letter of reference. Reference letters will only be requested from shortlisted \ncandidates.  \n• Provide required information regarding your Canadian work status and optional self-identification \nfor Affirmative Action purposes at this link: YUWorkAA.  \n• Direct questions about the position to Benedict Weobong, Search Committee Chair, \nbweobong@yorku.ca \n• Submit materials to: globsrch@yorku.ca, as a single PDF. \n \nLearn More About York: \n• York University generates and shares knowledge through our research, teaching and engagement \nwith communities around the world. The 2023-2028 Strategic Research Plan showcases the \ndepth, breadth and ambition of research at York. \n• York’s commitments to social justice are laid out in our Decolonizing, Equity, Diversity and \nInclusion Strategy, the Framework & Action Plan on Black Inclusion and the Indigenous \nFramework for York University. \n• Follow the activities and accomplishments of York’s faculty, students and staff on YFile. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nFirst Nations peoples have lived on this part of Turtle Island for millennia, stewarding the land, the water and all that con tributes to life in this \nregion. Today, the culture and presence of First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples enrich the lands and people of this territory. More than two \ncenturies ago, the Mississauga people welcomed settlers to this territory, providing sustenance and engaging in trade and com merce. Today, York \nUniversity campuses are located on the Toronto Purchase Treaty No. 13 lands and territory of the Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation and the \nlands of the Anishinabek of the Williams Treaties First Nations. Our campuses are also situated on the traditional territorie s of the Huron-Wendat \nand the Haudenosaunee. Treaty history is foundational, and it is our collective responsibility to honour the land, as we honour and respect those \nwho have gone before us, those who are here and those who have yet to come. We are grateful for the opportunity to be learnin g, working and \nthriving on this land, and we commit to learn the truth and be active in the process of reconciliation. "
    },
    "https://www.yorku.ca/unit/vpf/wp-content/uploads/sites/1070/2026/09/AMPD_VAAH_Sculp.pdf": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Sculpture (Studio Area)",
      "description": " \nAssistant Professor in Sculpture (Studio Area) \nDepartment of Visual Art and Art History, School of the Arts, Media, Performance and Design \n \nThe Department of Visual Art and Art History in the School of the Arts, Media, Performance and Design at York \nUniversity invites highly qualified applicants for an Assistant Professor position in Sculpture (Studio Area). This is \nan open tenure-track appointment to the Professorial Stream to commence July 1, 2027.  \n \nWe seek a practicing artist with a primary focus on sculpture. The ideal candidate will have a strong technical, \nconceptual, and historical grounding in the medium and its relation to broader contemporary cultural production \nand discourses.  \n \nThe Department of Visual Art and Art History is one of the leaders in art history and art practice in Canada. \nRecent renovations of undergraduate studio spaces, expansion of the MFA studios, and creation of a visual art \nstudy centre have contributed to the growth of the department. We are committed to teaching critical issues in \nart and art history, and to the ongoing development of programs in all areas of studio and studies.  \n \nCandidate Qualifications: \n• Degree: A PhD or PhD equivalent such as an MFA with a substantial record of professional experience, with \na focus on sculpture practice, by the appointment’s start is required. \n• Scholarship: \no A coherent and well-articulated program of research creation and specialization in contemporary \nsculptural practices, including but not limited to digital techniques and processes, ceramic sculpture, \nfoundry, public art, installation art practices, and all other forms of traditional and non-traditional \nsculpture. \no A record or evident promise of generating innovative, substantive, rigorous, and as appropriate, externally \nfunded research creation.  \no A record or evident promise of making influential contributions and demonstrating excellence in the field \n(e.g., regional, national, and international exhibitions and awards, commissions, reviews, collections). \n• Teaching: \no A record or evident promise of excellence in teaching and dedication to students (e.g., innovative course \nsyllabi, student and peer assessments, etc.). \no A minimum of two years post-secondary teaching is required. \no Suitability for prompt appointment to the Faculty of Graduate Studies, given that the position will involve \ngraduate teaching and supervision. \n• Additional: Through their teaching and practice-led research, applicants for this position must demonstrate \ntechnical expertise and conceptual rigour in sculptural processes that would enable them to teach both \nfoundational techniques and multi-disciplinary experimentation. \no The successful candidate will be able to integrate evolving digital technologies with contemporary fine \nart theory and practice. \no The successful candidate will join collaborative and committed peers who share administrative duties \nand responsibilities such as departmental governance and committee work. \n \nHiring Policies: \n• The anticipated starting salary for this position at the rank of Assistant Professor is in the range of $130,000 \nto $160,000 and will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. York University offers generous \nbenefits and salary provisions.  \n• All York University positions are subject to budgetary approval. \n• York is an Affirmative Action (AA) employer and strongly values diversity, including gender and sexual \ndiversity, in its community. Details of the AA Program, which applies to women, members of racialized \n\n \ngroups, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities and those who identify as 2SLGBTQ+, can be found \nhere or by contacting Aqsa Ahmed, EDI Program Manager (aqsa517@yorku.ca).  \n• York welcomes and employs scholars from all over the world. All qualified candidates are encouraged to \napply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority.  \n• York has a policy on Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities and is committed to \nworking towards a barrier-free workplace and expanding the accessibility of the workplace to persons with \ndisabilities. Applicants who require accommodation are invited to contact Patrick Legris, Administrative \nAssistant of the Department of Visual Art and Art History, vaah-search@yorku.ca. \n \nApplication Process: \n• Due date for completed applications: November 10, 2026 \n• Required materials: 1) up-to-date CV highlighting record of professional exhibitions, research presentations, \nawards and accolades; 2) portfolio of relevant work; 3) letter of application articulating your experience, \nresearch agenda and approach to teaching excellence, including accomplishments and pedagogical \ninnovations; 4) names and contact information of three professional references. \n• Provide required information regarding your Canadian work status and optional self-identification for \nAffirmative Action purposes at this link: YUWorkAA. \n• Direct questions about the position to Patrick Legris, Administrative Assistant of the Department of Visual \nArt and Art History, vaah-search@yorku.ca.  \n• Submit materials: to vaah-search@yorku.ca, as a single PDF no larger than 20 MB. \n \nLearn More About York: \n• York University generates and shares knowledge through our research, teaching and engagement with \ncommunities around the world. The 2023-2028 Strategic Research Plan showcases the depth, breadth and \nambition of research at York. \n• York’s commitments to social justice are laid out in our Decolonizing, Equity, Diversity and Inclusion \nStrategy, the Framework & Action Plan on Black Inclusion and the Indigenous Framework for York \nUniversity. \n• Follow the activities and accomplishments of York’s faculty, students and staff on YFile. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nFirst Nations peoples have lived on this part of Turtle Island for millennia, stewarding the land, the water and all that con tributes to life \nin this region. Today, the culture and presence of First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples enrich the lands and peo ple of this territory. \nMore than two centuries ago, the Mississauga people welcomed settlers to this territory, providing sustenance and engaging in  trade \nand commerce. Today, York University campuses are located on the Toronto Purchase Treaty No. 13 lands and territory of the \nMississaugas of the Credit First Nation and the lands of the Anishinabek of the Williams Treaties First Nations. Our campuses are also \nsituated on the traditional territories of the Huron-Wendat and the Haudenosaunee. Treaty history is foundational, and it is our \ncollective responsibility to honour the land, as we honour and respect those who have gone before us, those who are here and thos e \nwho have yet to come. We are grateful for the opportunity to be learning, working and thriving on this land, and we commit to  learn the \ntruth and be active in the process of reconciliation. "
    },
    "https://www.yorku.ca/unit/vpf/wp-content/uploads/sites/1070/2026/10/LSE_MECH_MechEng.pdf": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream in Mechanical Engineering",
      "description": " \nAssistant Professor, Teaching Stream in Mechanical Engineering \nDepartment of Mechanical Engineering, Lassonde School of Engineering \n \nThe Department of Mechanical Engineering in the Lassonde School of Engineering at York University invites \nhighly qualified applicants for an Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream position in Mechanical Engineering. \nThis is an open teaching-centred, tenure-track appointment to commence July 1, 2027. \n \nThe Department of Mechanical Engineering is at the forefront of developing technologies ranging from \nadvanced materials and digital twins to aerospace systems, clean energy technologies and sustainable \ntransportation. Established in 2012, the Lassonde School of Engineering, York University offers a broad \nrange of undergraduate and graduate programs to educate multidisciplinary problem solvers, critical \nthinkers, and entrepreneurs who understand creativity, communications, social responsibility, and cultural \ndiversity.  \n \nThe successful candidate will be expected to teach undergraduate courses with an emphasis on \nengineering design. They will also provide creative educational leadership in enhancing teaching and \nlearning through curricular and pedagogical innovation in the classroom and at the program level. The \nsuccessful candidate will be expected to contribute to the department at a committee and administrative \nlevel. \n \nCandidate Qualifications: \n• A BEng (or equivalent) and PhD in Mechanical Engineering, either in hand or near completion by the \nappointment’s start is required. \n• Strong commitment to effective and reflective pedagogy and student success, with the capacity to \nintegrate theoretical and practical perspectives into mechanical engineering teaching, learning, \ncurriculum, program design, and evidence of good judgement. Experience with engineering curriculum \ndevelopment including project-based learning, assessment of design outcomes, and collaboration with \ninternal and external stakeholders, and universal design approaches to teaching and assessment is \npreferred. \n• A record or evident promise of excellence in teaching at the university level and dedication to students \n(e.g. teaching accomplishments, pedagogical innovations, and official student and peer evaluations). \n• A record or evident promise of making valuable contributions through administrative and committee \nservice. \n• A record or evident promise of experience in engineering design education, including cornerstone and \ncapstone design, design pedagogy, and studio- or maker-based instruction. \n• A record or evident promise of experience integrating technology into engineering education, including \nthe effective and responsible use of artificial intelligence (AI) tools to enhance teaching, learning, and \nassessment. \n• Industry experience in engineering design, product development, and project management is an asset. \n• Candidates must hold, or be eligible to obtain, a Professional Engineering (or Limited) license in Canada \nshortly after their appointment.  \n \nHiring Policies: \n• The anticipated starting salary for this position at the rank of Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream is in \nthe range of $134,000 to $164,000 and will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. York \nUniversity offers generous benefits and salary provisions. \n\n \n• All York University positions are subject to budgetary approval. \n• York University is an Affirmative Action (AA) employer and strongly values diversity, including gender \nand sexual diversity, within its community. The AA Program, which applies to women, members of \nracialized groups, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities and those who self-identify as \n2SLGBTQ+, can be found here or by contacting Aqsa Ahmed, EDI Program Manager \n(aqsa517@yorku.ca). \n• York welcomes and employs scholars from all over the world. All qualified candidates are encouraged \nto apply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority. \n• York University has a policy on Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities and is \ncommitted to working towards a barrier-free workplace and to expanding the accessibility of the \nworkplace to persons with disabilities. Candidates who require accommodation during the selection \nprocess are invited to contact Dr. Aleksander Czekanski, Interim Chair, Department of Mechanical \nEngineering, mech_chair@lassonde.yorku.ca. \n• The use of artificial intelligence will be used to screen, assess or select applicants. Applicants who \nrequire more information are invited to contact Dr. Aleksander Czekanski, Interim Chair, Department of \nMechanical Engineering, mech_chair@lassonde.yorku.ca. \n \nApplication Components: \n• Due date for completed applications: November 15, 2026 \n• Required components: 1) current CV; 2) cover letter; 3) teaching dossier with evidence of excellence \nand innovation in teaching (e.g., sample course outlines, teaching evaluations, conference papers and \npublications relating to pedagogy). Candidates selected for further consideration will be asked to \nprovide names and contact details for three confirmed referees. Reference letters will only be \nrequested from shortlisted candidates. \n• Provide required information regarding your Canadian work status and optional self-identification for \nAffirmative Action purposes as part of the online application. \n• Direct questions about the position to Dr. Aleksander Czekanski, Interim Chair, Department of \nMechanical Engineering, mech_chair@lassonde.yorku.ca. \n• Submit materials: online at https://apply.interfolio.com/194697  \n \nUniversity Context: \n• York University generates and shares knowledge through our research, teaching and engagement with \ncommunities around the world. The 2023-2028 Strategic Research Plan showcases the depth, breadth \nand ambition of research at York. \n• York’s commitments to social justice are laid out in our Decolonizing, Equity, Diversity and Inclusion \nStrategy, the Framework & Action Plan on Black Inclusion and the Indigenous Framework for York \nUniversity. \n• Follow the activities and accomplishments of York’s faculty, students and staff on YFile. \n \n \nFirst Nations peoples have lived on this part of Turtle Island for millennia, stewarding the land, the water and all that con tributes to life in this region. \nToday, the culture and presence of First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples enrich the lands and people of this territory. More than two centuries ago, \nthe Mississauga people welcomed settlers to this territory, providing sustenance and engaging in trade and commerce. Today, Y ork University \ncampuses are located on the Toronto Purchase Treaty No. 13 lands and territory of the Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation and the lands of the \nAnishinabek of the Williams Treaties First Nations. Our campuses are also situated on the traditional territories of the Huro n-Wendat and the \nHaudenosaunee. Treaty history is foundational, and it is our collective responsibility to honour the land, as we honour and respect those who have \ngone before us, those who are here and those who have yet to come. We are grateful for the opportunity to be learning, workin g and thriving on this \nland, and we commit to learn the truth and be active in the process of reconciliation. "
    },
    "https://www.yorku.ca/unit/vpf/wp-content/uploads/sites/1070/2026/10/LSE_CIVIL_CivEng.pdf": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream in Structural Engineering/Structural Mechanics",
      "description": " \nAssistant Professor, Teaching Stream in Structural Engineering/Structural Mechanics \nDepartment of Civil Engineering, Lassonde School of Engineering \n \nThe Department of Civil Engineering in the Lassonde School of Engineering at York University invites \nhighly qualified applicants for an Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream position in Structural \nEngineering/Structural Materials. This is an open teaching-centred, tenure-track appointment to \ncommence July 1, 2027. \n \nThe Department of Civil Engineering is designing and building the systems we rely on every day, from \nresilient infrastructure and transportation networks to clean water solutions and sustainable \ncommunities. Established in 2012, the Lassonde School of Engineering, York University offers a broad \nrange of undergraduate and graduate programs to educate multidisciplinary problem solvers, critical \nthinkers, and entrepreneurs who understand creativity, communications, social responsibility, and \ncultural diversity. \n \nThe successful candidate will primarily be responsible for teaching and program development, with \nparticular emphasis on the development, enhancement, coordination and delivery of laboratory \npracticum components of undergraduate courses and possibly graduate courses, student capstone \nprojects, and common core engineering courses. The successful candidate will also contribute to \nsupporting student learning through pedagogical innovation, competency-based assessment, \ninteractions with industry, work-integrated learning opportunities, extracurricular activities, \ncompetitions, clubs, learning technologies, and collegial governance of the program. The successful \ncandidate will contribute to the continued advancement of the Department's educational programs \nand may have opportunities for programmatic leadership. The candidate will contribute to the service \nduties of the Department, Faculty and University level as is expected by all faculty members. \n \nCandidate Qualifications: \n• PhD in Structural Engineering/Structural Mechanics, or a closely related field, either in hand or \nnear completion by the appointment’s start, is required. \n• Strong commitment to pedagogy and student success, the capacity to bring a theoretical and \npractical orientation to Structural Engineering/Structural Mechanics teaching, learning and \nprogram design, and evidence of good judgment and a reflective approach to innovation in \nteaching. Experience with curriculum development including experiential education, technology-\nenhanced learning, structural modelling or structural monitoring, AI-based educational \ntechnologies and universal design approaches to teaching and assessment is preferred.  \n• A record or evident promise of excellence in teaching Structural Engineering/Structural Mechanics, \nMechanics of Materials, Computational Methods in Civil Engineering, Civil Engineering Capstone \nDesign, and related Civil Engineering and common core engineering courses, including but not \nlimited to ethics, creative problem solving, and communication, engineering design, project \nmanagement at the university level (e.g. teaching accomplishments, pedagogical innovations, \nstudent and peer evaluations, or other relevant evidence).  \n• A record of evident promise of making valuable contributions through administrative and \ncommittee service. \n\n \n• Candidates must hold or be eligible to obtain a Professional Engineering (P.Eng.) license in Canada \nshortly after their appointment. \n• Suitability for prompt appointment to the Faculty of Graduate Studies, given that the position will \ninvolve graduate teaching and supervision.  \n• A record or evident promise of experience or interest in curriculum and program development and \nenhancement will be considered an asset. Preference will be given to candidates with \ndemonstrated experience in accreditation and curriculum quality assurance processes. \n• A record or evident promise of potential to contribute to student engagement through industry \nengagement, work-integrated learning, extracurricular activities, competitions, and student clubs \nwill be considered an asset.  \n \nHiring Policies: \n• The anticipated starting salary for this position at the rank of Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream \nis in the range of $134,000 to $164,000 and will be commensurate with qualifications and \nexperience. York University offers generous benefits and salary provisions. \n• All York University positions are subject to budgetary approval. \n• York University is an Affirmative Action (AA) employer and strongly values diversity, including \ngender and sexual diversity, within its community. The AA Program, which applies to women, \nmembers of racialized groups, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities and those who self-\nidentify as 2SLGBTQ+, can be found here or by contacting Aqsa Ahmed, EDI Program Manager \n(aqsa517@yorku.ca). \n• York welcomes and employs scholars from all over the world. All qualified candidates are \nencouraged to apply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority.  \n• York University has a policy on Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities and is \ncommitted to working towards a barrier-free workplace and to expanding the accessibility of the \nworkplace to persons with disabilities. Candidates who require accommodation during the \nselection process are invited to contact Dr. Mojgan Jadidi, Chair, Department of Civil Engineering, \ncivil.chair@lassonde.yorku.ca. \n• The use of artificial intelligence will be used to screen, assess or select applicants. Applicants who \nrequire more information are invited to contact Dr. Mojgan Jadidi, Chair, Department of Civil \nEngineering, civil.chair@lassonde.yorku.ca. \n \nApplication Components: \n• Due date for completed applications: November 30, 2026 \nRequired components: 1) current CV; 2) cover letter; 3) teaching dossier which demonstrates \nexcellence and innovation and includes a statement on career achievements in the area of \nteaching, and any or all of the following: awards/honours; scholarly work related to pedagogy or \nany related training (include samples of most significant and relevant scholarly work conducted); \ninteractions with industry; supervising or advising student projects; relevant service or \nadministrative duties. Candidates selected for further consideration will be asked to provide \nnames and contact details for three confirmed referees. Reference letters will only be requested \nfrom shortlisted candidates.  \n• Provide required information regarding your Canadian work status and optional self-identification \nfor Affirmative Action purposes as part of the online application. \n \n• Direct questions about the position to Dr. Mojgan Jadidi, Chair, Department of Civil Engineering,  \ncivil.chair@lassonde.yorku.ca.  \n• Submit materials: online at https://apply.interfolio.com/194690  \n \nUniversity Context: \n• York University generates and shares knowledge through our research, teaching and engagement \nwith communities around the world. The 2023-2028 Strategic Research Plan showcases the \ndepth, breadth and ambition of research at York. \n• York’s commitments to social justice are laid out in our Decolonizing, Equity, Diversity and \nInclusion Strategy, the Framework & Action Plan on Black Inclusion and the Indigenous \nFramework for York University. \n• Follow the activities and accomplishments of York’s faculty, students and staff on YFile. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nFirst Nations peoples have lived on this part of Turtle Island for millennia, stewarding the land, the water and all that con tributes to life in this region. \nToday, the culture and presence of First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples enrich the lands and people of this territory. More than two centuries ago, \nthe Mississauga people welcomed settlers to this territory, providing sustenance and engaging in trade and commerce. Today, Y ork University \ncampuses are located on the Toronto Purchase Treaty No. 13 lands and territory of the Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation and the lands of the \nAnishinabek of the Williams Treaties First Nations. Our campuses are also situated on the traditional territories of the Huro n-Wendat and the \nHaudenosaunee. Treaty history is foundational, and it is our collective responsibility to honour the land, as we honour and respect those who have \ngone before us, those who are here and those who have yet to come. We are grateful for the opportunity to be learning, workin g and thriving on this \nland, and we commit to learn the truth and be active in the process of reconciliation. "
    },
    "https://www.yorku.ca/unit/vpf/wp-content/uploads/sites/1070/2026/10/OSG_BFIP.Law_.pdf": {
      "title": "Assistant and/or Associate Professor in Business Law, Family Law, Indigenous Law, or Private Law (4",
      "description": " \nAssistant and/or Associate Professor in Business Law, Family Law, Indigenous Law, or Private Law (4 \nPositions)  \nOsgoode Hall Law School, York University \n \nOsgoode Hall Law School at York University invites highly qualified applicants for 4 positions as an \nAssistant and/or Associate Professor in the areas of:  \n \n1) Business Law (with demonstrated expertise in corporate law);   \n \n2) Family Law (specifically focused on the fundamentals of family law with direct relevance and \nimpact in Canada, including the law governing marriage, divorce, child custody and support, \nproperty division, adoption, guardianship and family dispute resolution); or  \n \n3) Indigenous Law (specifically the law and legal traditions of Indigenous peoples in Canada. \nAlthough there is a preference for an Indigenous scholar, applications are welcomed from all \nqualified candidates); or \n \n4) Private Law (restricted to Contract Law and/or Tort Law).  \n \nWe expect that two of these appointments would be made at the Assistant Professor level and two at \neither the Assistant or Associate Professor level. These are open tenure-track appointments to the \nProfessorial Stream to commence July 1, 2027. \n \nOsgoode Hall Law School is one of Canada’s most distinguished and recognized legal institutions. For \nmore than a century, we have been preparing law students and future professionals. Osgoode’s \nmotto, Through Law to Justice, reflects our commitment to legal education and research in service of \nthe legal profession, Canada, and the wider world. \n \nOsgoode is currently finalizing an inclusive, consultative, and coordinated planning process for its \n2026-2030 Strategic Plan. The plan is organized around three primary areas of focus: \n \n1. Teaching & Learning: Championing a full spectrum of inspiring, relevant, and demanding legal \neducation, from JD to graduate to life-long professional development. \n2. Research & Knowledge: Producing, mobilizing, and recognizing rigorous, original, and \nconsequential legal scholarship that advances knowledge, policy development, innovation, and \nimpact. \n3. Osgoode’s People and Place in the World: Making a difference by connecting learning, \nresearch, and professional development with Osgoode's local and global partners and \ncommunities. \n \nOsgoode is committed to academic excellence, truth and reconciliation, equity and diversity, \ninclusion, and well-being across the full spectrum of its work. These commitments inform our \nteaching, research, community engagement, and the support we provide to students, faculty, and \nstaff. \n\n \nCandidate Qualifications: \n• Degree: All candidates must hold a JD degree and a graduate degree in law or equivalent \ncredentials. PhD or PhD near completion by the appointment’s start in law or a related field is \nstrongly preferred. \n• Scholarship: \no A coherent and well-articulated program of research, writing and knowledge dissemination. \no A record or evident promise of generating innovative, substantive, rigorous, and as appropriate, \nexternally funded research and/or writing. \no A record or evident promise of making influential contributions and demonstrating excellence of \na recognized international calibre in the field (e.g. clear research plan; a record of high-quality \npublications (or forthcoming publications), including with significant journals in the field; \npresentations at major conferences; awards and achievements; and strong recommendations \nfrom referees of high standing). \n• Teaching: \no A record or evident promise of excellence in teaching and dedication to students (e.g. a teaching \ndossier; teaching accomplishments and pedagogical innovations, including in high-priority areas \nsuch as experiential education and technology-enhanced learning; teaching evaluations; and \nstrong letters of reference). \no Suitability for prompt appointment to the Faculty of Graduate Studies, given that the positions \nwill involve graduate teaching and supervision. \n \nHiring Policies: \n•  The anticipated starting salary for this position at the rank of Assistant Professor is in the range of \n$115,000 to $145,000 and will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. The \nanticipated starting salary for this position at the rank of Associate Professor will be \ncommensurate with qualifications and experience. York University offers generous benefits and \nsalary provisions. \n• All York University positions are subject to budgetary approval. \n• Osgoode Hall Law School is committed to the principles of equality and diversity, and inclusion, \nbroadly understood. We are particularly interested in candidates with diverse backgrounds and \nespecially encourage candidates in equity, diversity and inclusion categories. As a part of this \ncommitment, the Law School’s Employment Equity Plan aims to increase the representation on \nfaculty of members from five groups: women, racialized persons, Indigenous persons, persons \nwith disabilities, and LGBTQ2+ persons. Persons identifying as members of these equity-seeking \ngroups are particularly encouraged to apply. \n• York welcomes and employs scholars from all over the world. All qualified candidates are \nencouraged to apply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority.  \nYork has a policy on Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities and is \ncommitted to working towards a barrier-free workplace and expanding the accessibility of the \nworkplace to persons with disabilities. Applicants who require accommodation are invited to \ncontact Adam Parachin, Chair of the Faculty Recruitment Committee,  \nfacultyrecruitment@osgoode.yorku.ca. \n \n \n \n \nApplication Process: \n• Due date for completed applications: November 7, 2026  \n• Required materials: 1) a completed Application Form; 2) current CV; 3) cover letter; 4) copies of \nlaw and graduate transcripts; 5) a detailed research plan; 6) a teaching dossier (see details below); \n7) copies of three academic articles or book chapters authored by the applicant (where published \nmaterials are not available, one or more dissertation/thesis chapter(s) may be submitted in lieu); \n8) contact information for three referees. Reference letters will only be requested from shortlisted \ncandidates All applications will be kept confidential to the Osgoode Faculty Recruitment \nCommittee and faculty colleagues who provide reviews of files.  \n• The teaching dossier should be no more than 15-20 pages and should include teaching \nevaluations, a statement of teaching philosophy, an account of teaching experiences and related \nresponsibilities (list of courses taught/course outlines, examples of print and digital teaching \nmaterials and how they are used), a summary of commitment to professional development \n(participation in teaching workshops; supervision of masters and doctoral students; mentoring \nnew faculty), and other evidence of teaching effectiveness (student letters of support; teaching \nawards). \n• Provide required information regarding your Canadian work status and optional self-identification \nfor Affirmative Action purposes at this link: YUWorkAA. \n• A process for verifying Indigenous belonging may be applied to candidates who identify as \nIndigenous.  \n• Direct questions about the position process or applications to Adam Parachin, Chair of the Faculty \nRecruitment Committee, at facultyrecruitment@osgoode.yorku.ca. \n• Submit materials to email: facultyrecruitment@osgoode.yorku.ca, as a single PDF. \n \nLearn More About York and Osgoode: \n• York University generates and shares knowledge through our research, teaching and engagement \nwith communities around the world. The 2023-2028 Strategic Research Plan showcases the \ndepth, breadth and ambition of research at York. \n• York’s commitments to social justice are laid out in our Decolonizing, Equity, Diversity and \nInclusion Strategy, the Framework & Action Plan on Black Inclusion and the Indigenous \nFramework for York University. \n• Follow the activities and accomplishments of York’s faculty, students and staff on YFile. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nFirst Nations peoples have lived on this part of Turtle Island for millennia, stewarding the land, the water and all that con tributes to life in this \nregion. Today, the culture and presence of First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples enrich the lands and people of this territory. More than two \ncenturies ago, the Mississauga people welcomed settlers to this territory, providing sustenance and engaging in trade and com merce. Today, York \nUniversity campuses are located on the Toronto Purchase Treaty No. 13 lands and territory of the Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation and the \nlands of the Anishinabek of the Williams Treaties First Nations. Our campuses are also situated on the traditional territorie s of the Huron-Wendat \nand the Haudenosaunee. Treaty history is foundational, and it is our collective responsibility to honour the land, as we honour and respect those \nwho have gone before us, those who are here and those who have yet to come. We are grateful for the opportunity to be learnin g, working and \nthriving on this land, and we commit to learn the truth and be active in the process of reconciliation. "
    },
    "https://www.yorku.ca/unit/vpf/wp-content/uploads/sites/1070/2026/10/AMPD_CRC_AI.pdf": {
      "title": "Canada Research Chair (Tier 2) Artificial Intelligence (AI) and the Arts (Assistant/Associate",
      "description": " \n \n \nCanada Research Chair (Tier 2) Artificial Intelligence (AI) and the Arts (Assistant/Associate \nProfessor) \nSchool of the Arts, Media, Performance & Design \n \nDate Posted: October 7, 2026 \nApplication Deadline: November 18, 2026 \n \nThe School of the Arts, Media, Performance & Design (AMPD) at York University invites highly \nqualified applicants for a Tier 2 Canada Research Chair (CRC) at the rank of Assistant or Associate \nProfessor in Artificial Intelligence (AI) and the Arts. This is an open tenure-track appointment to the \nProfessorial Stream to commence July 1, 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. The successful \ncandidate will be appointed to a home department best suited to their research and teaching \n(Department of Computational Arts, Department of Film & Media Arts, Department of Visual Art & \nArt History, Department of Theatre, Dance & Performance, Department of Music, Department of \nDesign) or joint appointment across two units. \nThrough a combined engagement with creative practice, ethical frameworks, and emerging \ntechnologies, the successful candidate will play a pivotal role in shaping how the Arts contribute to \nthe development and critical examination of AI. The successful candidate will provide leadership in \nnovel research creation that bridges Arts and STEM approaches and will deepen interdisciplinary \nsynergies across AMPD while fostering collaboration with communities, creative industries, and \nnational/international partners. The successful candidate will also be responsible for researching \nand developing courses on AI across the Arts. \n \nThe Canada Research Chairs (CRC) program seeks to attract outstanding researchers for careers at \nCanadian universities. Tier 2 Chairs are intended for exceptional emerging scholars (i.e., 10 years \nfrom their first independent academic research-related appointment at the time of nomination, \nwith consideration for career breaks) who have the acknowledged potential to lead their field of \nresearch. Appointment to a Tier 2 Chair is for five years, is renewable once, and comes with \nenhanced research support from the program. Applicants who are more than 10 years from their \nfirst independent academic research-related appointment (and where career breaks exist, \nincluding maternity leave, extended sick leave, etc.) may have their eligibility for a Tier 2 Canada \nResearch Chair assessed through the program’s Tier 2 justification process. Please see the CRC \nwebsite for further eligibility details. \nRecognizing the underrepresentation of women and gender minorities among CRCs, this position is \nopen only to qualified researchers who indicate on the Affirmative Action self-identification form \n(see below) that they identify as women or gender non-conforming (e.g., genderfluid, nonbinary, \nTwo-Spirit, trans man, trans woman). The appointment is part of the University’s Affirmative Action \nprogram, which is based on the special program provisions of the Ontario Human Rights Code.   \n \nIn alignment with the Tri-Agency Policy on Indigenous Citizenship and Membership Affirmation, \nYork University requires that Indigenous applicants provide supporting documentation to affirm \ntheir citizenship or membership to an Indigenous collective. This policy ensures that positions and \nfunding intended for the Indigenous chairs in the program benefit First Nations, Inuit, and Métis \n\napplicants. For more information regarding accepted supporting documentation, please consult the \nDirective on the Tri-Agency Policy on Indigenous Citizenship and Membership Affirmation.  \nYork acknowledges the potential impact of various life circumstances (e.g., illness, disability, \ncaregiving, family commitments) on a candidate’s record of research and other achievements. \nCandidates are encouraged to describe such circumstances and their impact in their application. \nThis information will be taken into careful consideration during the assessment process.   \nThis position is intended to recruit external candidates to add to the University’s research strength \nin the areas identified in this ad.  \nThis is a research-enhanced faculty position partially funded by the Connected Minds program, \nfunded in part by the Canada First Research Excellence Fund. The successful candidate will be \neligible for enhanced start-up funds. In addition, the successful candidate will engage with, and \nbenefit from, the Connected Minds program, which focuses on scholarship toward three main \nresearch goals: 1) to understand how the interplay of humans and intelligent technologies \nproduces unexpected, emergent properties at the community/whole society levels, 2) predict how \nnew technologies will disrupt the techno-social collective and 3) use these predictions to drive new \nresearch and technology development. The Connected Minds program is supported by a total of \n$318 million in funding over seven years (2023 – 2030).  \nCandidate Qualifications: 1 \n• Degree: PhD or PhD equivalent such as an MFA/MA with a substantial record of professional \nexperience, in a field relevant to creative activity in AI/emerging technologies by the \nappointment’s start is required.  \n• Creative Practice/Scholarship: \no A coherent and well-articulated program of research, research creation and/or professional \npractice and specialization in AI and Arts in areas such as studio-based AI research, emerging \ntechnologies and AI, interactive and immersive media, emerging technologies and \nperformance, artist-led machine learning, human-AI collaboration and creativity, community-\nengaged or ethical AI arts practices. \no A record or evident promise of generating innovative, substantive, rigorous, and as \nappropriate, externally funded research, research creation and/or creative practice. \no A record or evident promise of making influential contributions and demonstrating excellence \nin the field (e.g., statement and CV; publications; presentations in significant journals/venues \nin the field; presentations at major conferences/festivals; awards and accolades; strong \nrecommendations from referees of high standing). \n• Teaching: \no A record or evident promise of excellence in teaching and dedication to undergraduate \nstudents (e.g., the teaching statement; teaching evaluations; and evidence of teaching \naccomplishments and pedagogical innovations including in high-priority areas such as \nexperiential education, technology-enhanced learning and multi-platform approaches).  \no A record or evident promise of engaging in curriculum design and development. \no Suitability for prompt appointment to the Faculty of Graduate Studies, given that the \nposition will involve graduate teaching and supervision. \n \n \n1 Determined by the Hiring unit and York University, not the CRC program.  \nHiring Policies: \n• The anticipated starting salary for this position at the rank of Assistant Professor is in the range of \n$130,000 to $160,000, at the rank of Associate Professor is in the range of $138,000 to $168,000, \nand will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. York University offers generous \nbenefits and salary provisions.   \n• All York University positions are subject to budgetary approval. \n• York is an Affirmative Action (AA) employer and strongly values diversity, including gender and \nsexual diversity, in its community. Details of the AA Program, which applies to women, \nmembers of racialized groups, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities and those who \nidentify as 2SLGBTQ+, can be found here or by contacting Aqsa Ahmed, EDI Program Manager \n(aqsa517@yorku.ca).  \n• York welcomes and employs scholars from all over the world. All qualified candidates are \nencouraged to apply; however, Canadian citizens, permanent residents and Indigenous peoples \nof Canada will be given priority.  \n• York has a policy on Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities and is \ncommitted to working towards a barrier-free workplace and expanding the accessibility of the \nworkplace to persons with disabilities. Applicants who require accommodation are invited to \ncontact Sharon Hayashi, AMPD Associate Dean, Research, aiarts-search@yorku.ca. \n \nApplication Process: \n• Due date for completed applications: November 18, 2026.  \n• Required materials: 1) current CV; 2) a letter of application covering areas of interest in \nresearch/professional practice, teaching, and university service; 3) a statement of research or \nprofessional practice; 4) link to a digital portfolio of recent work/research-\ncreation/publications; 5) teaching statement and dossier (including teaching evaluations where \napplicable); 6) the names and contact information for three professional references. Reference \nletters will only be requested from shortlisted candidates. Provide required information \nregarding your Canadian work status and optional self-identification for Affirmative Action at \nthis link: YUWorkAA.  \n• Direct questions about the position to Sharon Hayashi, AMPD Associate Dean Research,  aiarts-\nsearch@yorku.ca. \nSubmit materials: to aiarts-search@yorku.ca, as a single PDF no larger than 20 MB.   \nLearn More About York and the School: \n• The School of the Arts, Media, Performance & Design (AMPD) at York University is Canada’s \nmost comprehensive art, culture, and design school. AMPD’s specialized facilities, studios, \nand creative spaces are connected to Toronto’s vibrant arts networks, media industries, \nand cultural communities. The dynamic network of researchers, artists, and \nscientists affiliated with Sensorium: Centre for Digital Art and Technology and the York \nUniversity Motion Media Studio at Cinespace are at the forefront of transforming artistic and \nscholarly practices, shaping the future of arts and culture, but also creating impactful \nconnections between people, technology, and the environment. York is a leading \ninternational teaching and research university, and a driving force for positive change. \nEmpowered by a welcoming and diverse community with a uniquely global perspective, we \nare preparing our students for their long-term careers and personal success. Together, we \ncan make things right for our communities, our planet, and our future. \n• York University generates and shares knowledge through our research, teaching and \nengagement with communities around the world. The 2023-2028 Strategic Research Plan \nshowcases the depth, breadth and ambition of research at York. \n• York’s commitments to social justice are laid out in our Decolonizing, Equity, Diversity and \nInclusion Strategy, the Framework & Action Plan on Black Inclusion and the Indigenous \nFramework for York University. \n• Follow the activities and accomplishments of York’s faculty, students and staff on YFile. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nFirst Nations peoples have lived on this part of Turtle Island for millennia, stewarding the land, the water and all that contributes to life in this region. \nToday, the culture and presence of First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples enrich the lands and people of this territory. More than two centuries ago, the \nMississauga people welcomed settlers to this territory, providing sustenance and engaging in trade and commerce. Today, York University campuses are \nlocated on the Toronto Purchase Treaty No. 13 lands and territory of the Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation and the lands of the Anishinabek of the \nWilliams Treaties First Nations. Our campuses are also situated on the traditional territories of the Huron-Wendat and the Haudenosaunee. Treaty history \nis foundational, and it is our collective responsibility to honour the land, as we honour and respect those who have gone before us, those who are here \nand those who have yet to come. We are grateful for the opportunity to be learning, working and thriving on this land, and we commit to learn the truth \nand be active in the process of reconciliation. \n "
    },
    "https://www.yorku.ca/unit/vpf/wp-content/uploads/sites/1070/2026/10/SCI_MATH_CRCT2.pdf": {
      "title": "Canada Research Chair (Tier 2) in Network Science (Assistant/Associate Professor)",
      "description": " \nCanada Research Chair (Tier 2) in Network Science (Assistant/Associate Professor) \nDepartment of Mathematics and Statistics, Faculty of Science \n \nDate Posted: October 1, 2026 \nApplication Deadline: November 1, 2026 \n \nThe Department of Mathematics and Statistics in the Faculty of Science at York University invites highly \nqualified applicants for a Tier 2 Canada Research Chair (CRC) at the rank of Assistant or Associate \nProfessor   in Network Science. This is an open tenure-track appointment to the Professorial Stream to \ncommence July 1, 2027.  \n \nThe Department of Mathematics and Statistics views this position as a key step towards establishing York \nUniversity as a Canadian leader in Network Science research and in post-doctoral, graduate, and \nundergraduate training. The successful candidate will also contribute to teaching and curriculum \ndevelopment, including to the existing BSc/BA program in Data Science. Our vision is for Network Science \nto bring together researchers across the university, graduate and undergraduate students, and industrial \npartners, to advance interdisciplinary and applied research. This position will strengthen student training in \nmethods for understanding complex networks and further enhance York University’s research reputation. \nThe Canada Research Chairs (CRC) program seeks to attract outstanding researchers for careers at \nCanadian universities. Tier 2 Chairs are intended for exceptional emerging scholars (i.e., 10 years from \ntheir first independent academic research-related appointment at the time of nomination, with \nconsideration for career breaks) who have the acknowledged potential to lead their field of research. \nAppointment to a Tier 2 Chair is for five years, is renewable once, and comes with enhanced research \nsupport from the program. Applicants who are more than 10 years from their first independent academic \nresearch-relate appointment (and where career breaks exist, including maternity leave, extended sick \nleave, etc.) may have their eligibility for a Tier 2 Canada Research Chair assessed through the \nprogram’s Tier 2 justification process. Please see the CRC website for further eligibility details.  \nRecognizing the underrepresentation of women and gender minorities among CRCs, this position is open \nonly to qualified researchers who indicate on the Affirmative Action self-identification form (see below) \nthat they identify as women or gender non-conforming (e.g., genderfluid, nonbinary, Two-Spirit, trans man, \ntrans woman). The appointment is part of the University’s Affirmative Action program, which is based on \nthe special program provisions of the Ontario Human Rights Code.    \nIn alignment with the Tri-Agency Policy on Indigenous Citizenship and Membership Affirmation, York \nUniversity requires that Indigenous applicants provide supporting documentation to affirm their citizenship \nor membership to an Indigenous collective. This policy ensures that positions and funding intended for the \nIndigenous chairs in the program benefit First Nations, Inuit, and Métis applicants. For more information \nregarding accepted supporting documentation, please consult the Directive on the Tri-Agency Policy on \nIndigenous Citizenship and Membership Affirmation.  \nYork acknowledges the potential impact of various life circumstances (e.g., illness, disability, caregiving, \nfamily commitments) on a candidate’s record of research and other achievements. Candidates are \n\n \nencouraged to describe such circumstances and their impact in their application. This information will be \ntaken into careful consideration during the assessment process.    \nThis position is intended to recruit external candidates to add to the University’s research strength in the \nareas identified in this ad.  \nThis is a research-enhanced faculty position partially funded by the Connected Minds program, funded in \npart by the Canada First Research Excellence Fund. The successful candidate will be eligible for enhanced \nstart-up funds. In addition, the successful candidate will engage with, and benefit from, the Connected \nMinds program, which focuses on scholarship toward three main research goals: 1) to understand how the \ninterplay of humans and intelligent technologies produces unexpected, emergent properties at the \ncommunity/whole society levels, 2) predict how new technologies will disrupt the techno-social collective \nand 3) use these predictions to drive new research and technology development. The Connected Minds \nprogram is supported by a total of $318 million in funding over seven years (2023 – 2030).  \nCandidate Qualifications: 1 \n• Degree: PhD or PhD near completion by the appointment’s start in a field closely related to Network \nScience, is required.  \n• Scholarship: \no A coherent and well-articulated program of research and specialization in any area of Network \nScience which may include but is not limited to the study of complex networks such as \ntelecommunication networks, computer networks, biological networks, cognitive and semantic \nnetworks, and social networks, using theories and methods such as graph theory, random matrix \ntheory, machine learning for graph-structured data, and inferential modeling.  \no A record or evident promise of generating innovative, substantive, rigorous, and as appropriate, \nexternally funded research.  \no A record or evident promise of making influential contributions and demonstrating excellence in the \nfield (e.g., a record of publications (or forthcoming publications) in top-ranked and field-relevant \njournals; presentations at major conferences; awards and accolades; and strong recommendations \nfrom referees of high standing). \n• Teaching: \no A record or evident promise of excellence in undergraduate teaching and dedication to students (e.g., \nteaching statement; teaching accomplishments and pedagogical innovations (e.g., high-priority areas \nsuch as experiential education and technology enhanced learning); student evaluations; strong \nletters of reference).  \no Suitability for prompt appointment to the Faculty of Graduate Studies, given that the position will \ninvolve graduate teaching and supervision. \n \nHiring Policies: \n• The anticipated starting salary for this position at the rank of Assistant Professor is in the range of \n$136,000 to $166,000, at the rank of Associate Professor is in the range of $147,000 to $177,000 and \nwill be commensurate with qualifications and experience. York University offers generous benefits and \nsalary provisions.  \n• All York University positions are subject to budgetary approval. \n \n1 Determined by the Hiring unit and York University, not the CRC program. \n \n• York is an Affirmative Action (AA) employer and strongly values diversity, including gender and sexual \ndiversity, in its community. Details of the AA Program, which applies to women, members of racialized \ngroups, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities and those who identify as 2SLGBTQ+, can be \nfound here or by contacting Aqsa Ahmed, EDI Program Manager (aqsa517@yorku.ca).  \n• York welcomes and employs scholars from all over the world. All qualified candidates are encouraged \nto apply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority.  \n• York has a policy on Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities and is committed to \nworking towards a barrier-free workplace and expanding the accessibility of the workplace to persons \nwith disabilities. Applicants who require accommodation are invited to contact Paul Skoufranis, Chair of \nthe Hiring Committee, at pskoufra@yorku.ca. \n• The use of artificial intelligence will be used to screen, assess or select applicants. Applicants who \nrequire more information are invited to contact Paul Skoufranis, Chair of the Hiring Committee, at \npskoufra@yorku.ca. \n \nApplication Process: \n• Due date for completed applications: November 1, 2026 \n• Required materials: 1) cover letter; 2) current CV; 3) research statement; 4) teaching statement; 5) \nteaching dossier; and 6) names and contact information for three referees willing to provide a \nconfidential letter of reference. Reference letters will only be requested from shortlisted candidates. \n• Provide required information regarding your Canadian work status and optional self-identification for \nAffirmative Action purposes as part of the online application at this link: YUWorkAA. \n• Direct questions about the position to Paul Skoufranis at pskoufra@yorku.ca. \n• Submit materials: at https://www.mathjobs.org/jobs/list/29106  \n \nLearn More About York: \n• York University generates and shares knowledge through our research, teaching and engagement with \ncommunities around the world. The 2023-2028 Strategic Research Plan showcases the depth, breadth \nand ambition of research at York. \n• York’s commitments to social justice are laid out in our Decolonizing, Equity, Diversity and Inclusion \nStrategy, the Framework & Action Plan on Black Inclusion and the Indigenous Framework for York \nUniversity. \n• Follow the activities and accomplishments of York’s faculty, students and staff on YFile. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nFirst Nations peoples have lived on this part of Turtle Island for millennia, stewarding the land, the water and all that con tributes to life \nin this region. Today, the culture and presence of First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples enrich the lands and peo ple of this territory. \nMore than two centuries ago, the Mississauga people welcomed settlers to this territory, providing sustenance and engaging in  trade \nand commerce. Today, York University campuses are located on the Toronto Purchase Treaty No. 13 lands and territory of the \nMississaugas of the Credit First Nation and the lands of the Anishinabek of the Williams Treaties First Nations. Our campuses  are also \nsituated on the traditional territories of the Huron-Wendat and the Haudenosaunee. Treaty history is foundational, and it is our \ncollective responsibility to honour the land, as we honour and respect those who have gone before us, those who are here and those \nwho have yet to come. We are grateful for the opportunity to be learning, working and thriving on this land, and we commit to  learn the \ntruth and be active in the process of reconciliation. "
    },
    "https://www.yorku.ca/unit/vpf/wp-content/uploads/sites/1070/2026/10/OSG_OsChair.pdf": {
      "title": "Full Professor and Osler Chair in Business Law",
      "description": " \nFull Professor and Osler Chair in Business Law \nOsgoode Hall Law School, York University \n \nOsgoode Hall Law School at York University invites highly qualified applicants for a Full Professor \nposition for the Osler Chair in Business Law. The Chair is for a term of five years, with the possibility of \nrenewal for an additional five-year period. The successful applicant is expected to have the necessary \nqualifications to be appointed as a professorial tenured professor. This is an open tenure-track \nappointment to the Professorial Stream to commence July 1, 2027. \n \nThe Osler Chair in Business Law is intended for an outstanding and established scholar. Fields of \nspecialization for the Chair are corporate law, corporate governance, regulation of competition, \nsecurities regulation, business transactions, commercial law, banking law, and trade regulation. \nPreferred candidates for the Chair will be expected to provide leadership in teaching and research in \nthe area of business law and be widely recognized in the business law field with a substantial record \nof original and significant published research. \n \nThe Chair is open to both internal York University and external candidates. External candidates must \nsatisfy the requirements for a tenured appointment at Osgoode Hall Law School in addition to the \nrequirements for appointment to the Chair. At the conclusion of the term of the Chair, where the \nChairholder was an external candidate, they will continue to hold a full-time tenured appointment at \nOsgoode Hall Law School. \n \nOsgoode Hall Law School is one of Canada’s most distinguished and recognized legal institutions. For \nmore than a century, we have been preparing law students and future professionals. Osgoode’s \nmotto, Through Law to Justice, reflects our commitment to legal education and research in service of \nthe legal profession, Canada, and the wider world. \n \nOsgoode is currently finalizing an inclusive, consultative, and coordinated planning process for its \n2026-2030 Strategic Plan. The plan is organized around three primary areas of focus: \n \n1. Teaching & Learning: Championing a full spectrum of inspiring, relevant, and demanding legal \neducation, from JD to graduate to life-long professional development. \n2. Research & Knowledge: Producing, mobilizing, and recognizing rigorous, original, and \nconsequential legal scholarship that advances knowledge, policy development, innovation, and \nimpact. \n3. Osgoode’s People and Place in the World: Making a difference by connecting learning, \nresearch, and professional development with Osgoode's local and global partners and \ncommunities. \n \nOsgoode is committed to academic excellence, truth and reconciliation, equity and diversity, \ninclusion, and well-being across the full spectrum of its work. These commitments inform our \nteaching, research, community engagement, and the support we provide to students, faculty, and \nstaff. \n \n\n \nCandidate Qualifications: \n• Degree: All candidates must hold a JD degree and a graduate degree in law or equivalent \ncredentials. PhD or PhD near completion by the appointment’s start in law or a related field is \nstrongly preferred. \n• Scholarship: \no A coherent and well-articulated program of research, writing and knowledge dissemination. \no A record of generating innovative, substantive, rigorous, and as appropriate, externally funded \nresearch and/or writing. \no A record of making influential contributions and demonstrating excellence of a recognized \ninternational calibre in the field (e.g. clear research plan; a record of high-quality publications (or \nforthcoming publications), including with significant journals in the field; presentations at major \nconferences; awards and achievements; and strong recommendations from referees of high \nstanding). \n• Teaching: \no A record of excellence in teaching and dedication to students (e.g. a teaching dossier; teaching \naccomplishments and pedagogical innovations, including in high priority areas such as \nexperiential education and technology-enhanced learning; teaching evaluations; and strong \nletters of reference). \no Suitability for prompt appointment to the Faculty of Graduate Studies, given that the position will \ninvolve graduate teaching and supervision. \n \nHiring Policies: \n• Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. York University offers generous \nbenefits and salary provisions. \n• All York University positions are subject to budgetary approval. \n• Osgoode Hall Law School is committed to the principles of equality and diversity, and inclusion, \nbroadly understood. We are particularly interested in candidates with diverse backgrounds and \nespecially encourage candidates in equity, diversity and inclusion categories. As a part of this \ncommitment, the Law School’s Employment Equity Plan aims to increase the representation on \nfaculty of members from five groups: women, racialized persons, Indigenous persons, persons \nwith disabilities, and LGBTQ2+ persons. Persons identifying as members of these equity-seeking \ngroups are particularly encouraged to apply. \n• York welcomes and employs scholars from all over the world. All qualified candidates are \nencouraged to apply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority.  \nYork has a policy on Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities and is \ncommitted to working towards a barrier-free workplace and expanding the accessibility of the \nworkplace to persons with disabilities. Applicants who require accommodation are invited to \ncontact Adam Parachin, Chair of the Faculty Recruitment Committee,  \nfacultyrecruitment@osgoode.yorku.ca. \n \nApplication Process: \n• Due date for completed applications: November 7, 2026  \n• Required materials: 1) a completed Application Form; 2) current CV; 3) cover letter; 4) copies of \nlaw and graduate transcripts; 5) a detailed research plan; 6) a teaching dossier (see details \nbelow);7) copies of three academic articles or book chapters authored by the applicant (where \n \npublished materials are not available, one or more dissertation/thesis chapter(s) may be \nsubmitted in lieu); 7) contact information for three referees. Reference letters will only be \nrequested from shortlisted candidates directly to Osgoode. All applications will be kept \nconfidential to the Osgoode Faculty Recruitment Committee and faculty colleagues who provide \nreviews of files.   \n• The teaching dossier should be no more than 15-20 pages and should include teaching \nevaluations, a statement of teaching philosophy, an account of teaching experiences and related \nresponsibilities (list of courses taught/course outlines, examples of print and digital teaching \nmaterials and how they are used), a summary of commitment to professional development \n(participation in teaching workshops; supervision of masters and doctoral students; mentoring \nnew faculty), and other evidence of teaching effectiveness (student letters of support; teaching \nawards). \n• Provide required information regarding your Canadian work status and optional self-identification \nfor Affirmative Action purposes at this link: YUWorkAA.  \n• A process for verifying Indigenous belonging may be applied to candidates who identify as \nIndigenous.  \n• Direct questions about the position process or applications to Adam Parachin, Chair of the Faculty \nRecruitment Committee, at facultyrecruitment@osgoode.yorku.ca. \n• Submit materials to email: facultyrecruitment@osgoode.yorku.ca, as a single PDF. \n \nLearn More About York and Osgoode: \n• York University generates and shares knowledge through our research, teaching and engagement \nwith communities around the world. The 2023-2028 Strategic Research Plan showcases the \ndepth, breadth and ambition of research at York. \n• York’s commitments to social justice are laid out in our Decolonizing, Equity, Diversity and \nInclusion Strategy, the Framework & Action Plan on Black Inclusion and the Indigenous \nFramework for York University. \n• Follow the activities and accomplishments of York’s faculty, students and staff on YFile. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nFirst Nations peoples have lived on this part of Turtle Island for millennia, stewarding the land, the water and all that con tributes to life in this \nregion. Today, the culture and presence of First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples enrich the lands and people of this territory. More than two \ncenturies ago, the Mississauga people welcomed settlers to this territory, providing sustenance and engaging in trade and com merce. Today, York \nUniversity campuses are located on the Toronto Purchase Treaty No. 13 lands and territory of the Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation and the \nlands of the Anishinabek of the Williams Treaties First Nations. Our campuses are also situated on the traditional territorie s of the Huron-Wendat \nand the Haudenosaunee. Treaty history is foundational, and it is our collective responsibility to honour the land, as we honour and respect those \nwho have gone before us, those who are here and those who have yet to come. We are grateful for the opportunity to be learnin g, working and \nthriving on this land, and we commit to learn the truth and be active in the process of reconciliation. "
    },
    "https://www.yorku.ca/unit/vpf/wp-content/uploads/sites/1070/2026/10/LSE_CIVIL_CLA.pdf": {
      "title": "Sessional Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream in Civil Engineering",
      "description": " \nSessional Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream in Civil Engineering \nDepartment of Civil Engineering, Lassonde School of Engineering \n \nThe Department of Civil Engineering in the Lassonde School of Engineering at York University invites \nhighly qualified applicants for a Sessional Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream position in Civil \nEngineering. This is an open Contractually Limited Appointment (CLA) to the teaching stream for a \nthree-year term to commence July 1, 2027. \n \nThe Department of Civil Engineering is designing and building the systems we rely on every day, from \nresilient infrastructure and transportation networks to clean water solutions and sustainable \ncommunities. Established in 2012, the Lassonde School of Engineering, York University offers a broad \nrange of undergraduate and graduate programs to educate multidisciplinary problem solvers, critical \nthinkers, and entrepreneurs who understand creativity, communications, social responsibility, and \ncultural diversity. \n \nThe successful candidate will primarily be responsible for teaching and program development, with \nparticular emphasis on the development, enhancement, coordination and delivery of laboratory \npracticum components of undergraduate courses (and possibly graduate courses), student capstone \nprojects, and common core engineering courses. The successful candidate will also contribute to \nsupporting student learning through pedagogical innovation, competency-based assessment, \ninteractions with industry, learning technologies, and collegial governance of the program. The \nsuccessful candidate will contribute to the continued advancement of the Department's educational \nprograms and may have opportunities for programmatic leadership. \n \n Candidate Qualifications: \n• Degree: PhD or PhD near completion in Civil Engineering by the appointment’s start, with a \nsubstantial record of professional experience is required. \n• Teaching: \no Strong knowledge of core areas of Civil Engineering, including engineering mechanics, strength \nof materials, fluid mechanics, hydraulics, soil mechanics, foundations, water and wastewater \ntreatment, and highway materials, as well as common core engineering subjects such as \nengineering ethics, creative problem solving and communication, engineering design, and \nproject management. Experience teaching related areas, such as transportation planning and \nengineering, sustainable infrastructure, asset and project management, and/or advanced and \nemerging civil engineering technologies, will be considered an asset. \no A record or evident demonstrated commitment to, or potential for, contributing to the \ndevelopment and enhancement of educational programs, including curriculum development, \ncoordination, accreditation, and curriculum quality assurance processes will be considered an \nasset.  \no Experience with accreditation and curriculum quality assurance processes will be considered \nan asset.  \no A record or evident promise of making valuable contributions through administrative and \ncommittee service.  \n\n \no A record or evident promise of excellence in teaching and dedication to students at the \nuniversity level (e.g. teaching accomplishments, pedagogical innovations, student and peer \nevaluations, or other relevant evidence). \no A record or evident promise of ability or potential to develop and deliver courses with significant \nlaboratory practicum components.  \no Ability to teach in both in-person and blended/remote settings and across the fall, winter, and \nsummer terms, as required. \no Strong commitment to innovative pedagogy, student success, and competency-based \nassessment. \no Capacity to bring both theoretical and practical perspectives to Civil Engineering teaching. \n• Additional: Candidates must hold or be eligible to obtain a Professional Engineering (P.Eng.) \nlicense in Canada shortly after their appointment. \n \nHiring Policies: \n• The anticipated starting salary for this position at the rank of Sessional Assistant Professor, \nTeaching Stream is in the range of $125,000 to $155,000 and will be commensurate with \nqualifications and experience. York University offers generous benefits and salary provisions.  \n• All York University positions are subject to budgetary approval. \n• York is an Affirmative Action (AA) employer and strongly values diversity, including gender and \nsexual diversity, in its community. Details of the AA Program, which applies to women, members \nof racialized groups, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities and those who identify as \n2SLGBTQ+, can be found here or by contacting Aqsa Ahmed, EDI Program Manager \n(aqsa517@yorku.ca).  \n• York welcomes and employs scholars from all over the world. All qualified candidates are \nencouraged to apply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority.  \n• York has a policy on Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities and is \ncommitted to working towards a barrier-free workplace and expanding the accessibility of the \nworkplace to persons with disabilities. Applicants who require accommodation are invited to \ncontact Dr. Mojgan Jadidi, Chair, Department of Civil Engineering, civil.chair@lassonde.yorku.ca. \n• The use of artificial intelligence will be used to screen, assess or select applicants. Applicants who \nrequire more information are invited to contact Dr. Mojgan Jadidi, Chair, Department of Civil \nEngineering, civil.chair@lassonde.yorku.ca. \n \nApplication Process: \n• Due date for completed applications: November 30, 2026. \n• Required materials: 1) current CV; 2) a cover letter; 3) a teaching dossier including a statement \noutlining teaching philosophy and contributions to teaching (and curriculum development, if \napplicable; Candidates selected for further consideration will be asked to provide names and \ncontact details for three confirmed referees. Reference letters will only be requested from \nshortlisted candidates. \n• Provide required information regarding your Canadian work status and optional self-identification \nfor Affirmative Action purposes as part of the online application.  \n• Direct questions about the position to Dr. Mojgan Jadidi, Chair, Department of Civil Engineering at \ncivil.chair@lassonde.yorku.ca. \n• Submit materials: online at https://apply.interfolio.com/194686   \n \n \nLearn More About York: \n• York University generates and shares knowledge through our research, teaching and engagement \nwith communities around the world. The 2023-2028 Strategic Research Plan showcases the \ndepth, breadth and ambition of research at York. \n• York’s commitments to social justice are laid out in our Decolonizing, Equity, Diversity and \nInclusion Strategy, the Framework & Action Plan on Black Inclusion and the Indigenous \nFramework for York University. \n• Follow the activities and accomplishments of York’s faculty, students and staff on YFile. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nFirst Nations peoples have lived on this part of Turtle Island for millennia, stewarding the land, the water and all that con tributes to life in this \nregion. Today, the culture and presence of First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples enrich the lands and people of this territory. More than two \ncenturies ago, the Mississauga people welcomed settlers to this territory, providing sustenance and engaging in trade and com merce. Today, York \nUniversity campuses are located on the Toronto Purchase Treaty No. 13 lands and territory of the Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation and the \nlands of the Anishinabek of the Williams Treaties First Nations. Our campuses are also situated on the traditional territorie s of the Huron-Wendat \nand the Haudenosaunee. Treaty history is foundational, and it is our collective responsibility to honour the land, as we honour and respect those \nwho have gone before us, those who are here and those who have yet to come. We are grateful for the opportunity to be learnin g, working and \nthriving on this land, and we commit to learn the truth and be active in the process of reconciliation. "
    },
    "https://www.yorku.ca/unit/vpf/wp-content/uploads/sites/1070/2026/10/HH_PSYC_CLA.pdf": {
      "title": "Sessional Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream in Statistical Methods",
      "description": " \nSessional Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream in Statistical Methods \nDepartment of Psychology, Faculty of Health \n \nThe Department of Psychology and the Faculty of Health at York University invites highly qualified \napplicants for a Sessional Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream position in Statistical Methods. This is \nan open Contractually Limited Appointment (CLA) to the teaching stream for a three-year (3) term to \ncommence July 1, 2027. \n \nYork is a leading international teaching and research university, and a driving force for positive change. \nEmpowered by a welcoming and diverse community with a uniquely global perspective, we are \npreparing our students for their long-term careers and personal success. Together, we can make \nthings right for our communities, our planet and our future. Our Psychology Department is ranked 8th \nin Canada according to the 2020 QS World Rankings and 33rd in the World according to the 2020 \nTimes Higher Education Global Impact Ranking. The successful candidate’s core responsibilities will \nbe teaching introductory and intermediate undergraduate statistics and research methods. \n \nCandidate Qualifications: \n• Degree: PhD in Psychology by the appointment’s start date, is required.  \n• Teaching: \no A record or evident promise of excellence in teaching Statistical Methods and dedication to \nstudents (e.g. teaching statement; teaching accomplishments and pedagogical innovations \nincluding in high-priority areas such as experiential education and technology enhanced \nlearning; teaching evaluations; and strong letters of reference). \no A record or evident promise of excellence in teaching university level courses in Statistical \nMethods for psychology; \no A record or evident promise of a strong commitment to pedagogy and student success, the \ncapacity to bring a theoretical and practical orientation to Statistical Methods teaching and \nlearning and demonstrate good judgment and a reflective approach to innovation in teaching. \n \nHiring Policies: \n• The anticipated starting salary for this position at the rank of Sessional Assistant Professor, \nTeaching Stream is in the range of $132,000 to $162,000 and will be commensurate with \nqualifications and experience. York University offers generous benefits and salary provisions.  \n• All York University positions are subject to budgetary approval. \n• York is an Affirmative Action (AA) employer and strongly values diversity, including gender and \nsexual diversity, in its community. Details of the AA Program, which applies to women, members \nof racialized groups, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities and those who identify as \n2SLGBTQ+, can be found here or by contacting Aqsa Ahmed, EDI Program Manager \n(aqsa517@yorku.ca).  \n• York welcomes and employs scholars from all over the world. All qualified candidates are \nencouraged to apply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority.  \n• York has a policy on Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities and is \ncommitted to working towards a barrier-free workplace and expanding the accessibility of the \n\n \nworkplace to persons with disabilities. Applicants who require accommodation are invited to \ncontact Jessica Sutherland, Committee Chair, at psycjobs@yorku.ca.  \n• The use of artificial intelligence may be used to screen, assess or select applicants. Applicants \nwho require more information are invited to contact Jessica Sutherland, Committee Chair, \npsycjobs@yorku.ca.  \n \nApplication Process: \n• Due date for completed applications: November 20, 2026 \n• Required Materials: 1) cover letter of application (please specify the title of the position in the \nsubject line of your email and in any correspondence); 2) an up-to-date curriculum vitae; 3) a \nteaching dossier (that includes a teaching statement, course outlines from previously taught \ncourses, and teaching evaluations); and 4) the names and contact information for three referees \nwilling to provide a confidential letter of reference. Reference letters will only be requested from \nshortlisted candidates. Referees for short-listed for candidates should be prepared to provide \nstrong letters of reference and be advised to address the candidate’s qualifications and \nexperience in relation to the position. \n• Provide required information regarding your Canadian work status and optional self-identification \nfor Affirmative Action purposes at this link: YUWorkAA. \n• Direct questions about the position to Jessica Sutherland, Committee Chair, at \npsycjobs@yorku.ca. \n• Submit materials to: psycjobs@yorku.ca, as a single PDF. \n \nLearn More About York: \n• York University generates and shares knowledge through our research, teaching and engagement \nwith communities around the world. The 2023-2028 Strategic Research Plan showcases the \ndepth, breadth and ambition of research at York. \n• York’s commitments to social justice are laid out in our Decolonizing, Equity, Diversity and \nInclusion Strategy, the Framework & Action Plan on Black Inclusion and the Indigenous \nFramework for York University. \n• Follow the activities and accomplishments of York’s faculty, students and staff on YFile. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nFirst Nations peoples have lived on this part of Turtle Island for millennia, stewarding the land, the water and all that con tributes to life in this \nregion. Today, the culture and presence of First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples enrich the lands and people of this territory. More than two \ncenturies ago, the Mississauga people welcomed settlers to this territory, providing sustenance and engaging in trade and com merce. Today, York \nUniversity campuses are located on the Toronto Purchase Treaty No. 13 lands and territory of the Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation and the \nlands of the Anishinabek of the Williams Treaties First Nations. Our campuses are also situated on the traditional territorie s of the Huron-Wendat \nand the Haudenosaunee. Treaty history is foundational, and it is our collective responsibility to honour the land, as we honour and respect those \nwho have gone before us, those who are here and those who have yet to come. We are grateful for the opportunity to be learnin g, working and \nthriving on this land, and we commit to learn the truth and be active in the process of reconciliation. "
    },
    "https://www.yorku.ca/unit/vpf/wp-content/uploads/sites/1070/2026/10/LSE_MECH_CLA.pdf": {
      "title": "Sessional Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream in Thermofluids or Mechatronics and Automation",
      "description": " \nSessional Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream in Thermofluids or Mechatronics and Automation \nDepartment of Mechanical Engineering, Lassonde School of Engineering \n \nThe Department of Mechanical Engineering in the Lassonde School of Engineering at York University \ninvites highly qualified applicants for a Sessional Assistant Professor, Teaching Stream in \nThermofluids or Mechatronics and Automation. This is an open Contractually Limited Appointment \n(CLA) to the teaching stream for a 3-year term to commence July 1, 2027. \n \nThe Department of Mechanical Engineering is at the forefront of developing technologies ranging from \nadvanced materials and digital twins to aerospace systems, clean energy technologies and \nsustainable transportation. Established in 2012, the Lassonde School of Engineering, York University \noffers a broad range of undergraduate and graduate programs to educate multidisciplinary problem \nsolvers, critical thinkers, and entrepreneurs who understand creativity, communications, social \nresponsibility, and cultural diversity.  \n \nCandidate Qualifications: \n• Degree: A BEng (or equivalent) and PhD or PhD near completion in Mechanical Engineering by the \nappointment’s start is required.  \n• Teaching: \no A record or evident promise of excellence in teaching and dedication to students (e.g. teaching \naccomplishments, pedagogical innovations, and official student and peer evaluations).  \no A strong commitment to effective and reflective pedagogy, with the capacity to bring theoretical \nand practical perspectives to mechanical engineering teaching and learning. A relevant teaching \nqualification is an asset. \no Experience in teaching Thermofluids subjects such as thermodynamics, heat transfer, fluid \nmechanics OR Mechatronics and Automation subjects such as control systems, robotics, \ninstrumentation, sensors and actuators, and industrial automation. \no A record or evident promise of making valuable contributions through administrative and \ncommittee service. \no Excellent organizational and communication skills, with the ability to work effectively with peers, \nteaching assistants, and students. \n• Additional: Candidates must hold, or be eligible to obtain, a Professional Engineering (or Limited) \nlicense in Canada shortly after their appointment. \n \nHiring Policies: \n• The anticipated starting salary for this position at the rank of Sessional Assistant Professor, \nTeaching Stream, is in the range of $125,000 to $155,000 and will be commensurate with \nqualifications and experience. York University offers generous benefits and salary provisions. York \nUniversity offers generous benefits and salary provisions. \n• All York University positions are subject to budgetary approval. \n• York is an Affirmative Action (AA) employer and strongly values diversity, including gender and \nsexual diversity, in its community. Details of the AA Program, which applies to women, members \nof racialized groups, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities and those who identify as \n2SLGBTQ+, can be found here or by contacting Aqsa Ahmed, EDI Program Manager \n(aqsa517@yorku.ca).  \n\n \n• York welcomes and employs scholars from all over the world. All qualified candidates are \nencouraged to apply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority.  \n• York has a policy on Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities and is \ncommitted to working towards a barrier-free workplace and expanding the accessibility of the \nworkplace to persons with disabilities. Applicants who require accommodation are invited to \ncontact Dr. Aleksander Czekanski, Interim Chair, Department of Mechanical Engineering, \nmech_chair@lassonde.yorku.ca. \n• The use of artificial intelligence will be used to screen, assess or select applicants. Applicants who \nrequire more information are invited to contact Dr. Aleksander Czekanski, Interim Chair, \nDepartment of Mechanical Engineering, mech_chair@lassonde.yorku.ca. \n \nApplication Process: \n• Due date for completed applications: November 15, 2026 \nRequired materials: 1) current CV; 2) cover letter; 3) teaching dossier with evidence of excellence \nand innovation in teaching (e.g., sample course outlines, teaching evaluations, conference papers \nand publications relating to pedagogy). Candidates selected for further consideration will be asked \nto provide names and contact details for three confirmed referees. Reference letters will only be \nrequested from shortlisted candidates. \n• Provide required information regarding your Canadian work status and optional self-identification \nfor Affirmative Action purposes as part of the online application. \n• Direct questions about the position to Dr. Aleksander Czekanski, Interim Chair, Department of \nMechanical Engineering, mech_chair@lassonde.yorku.ca. \n• Submit materials: online at https://apply.interfolio.com/194695   \n \nLearn More About York: \n• York University generates and shares knowledge through our research, teaching and engagement \nwith communities around the world. The 2023-2028 Strategic Research Plan showcases the \ndepth, breadth and ambition of research at York. \n• York’s commitments to social justice are laid out in our Decolonizing, Equity, Diversity and \nInclusion Strategy, the Framework & Action Plan on Black Inclusion and the Indigenous \nFramework for York University. \n• Follow the activities and accomplishments of York’s faculty, students and staff on YFile. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nFirst Nations peoples have lived on this part of Turtle Island for millennia, stewarding the land, the water and all that con tributes to life in this \nregion. Today, the culture and presence of First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples enrich the lands and people of this territory. More than two \ncenturies ago, the Mississauga people welcomed settlers to this territory, providing sustenance and engaging in trade and com merce. Today, York \nUniversity campuses are located on the Toronto Purchase Treaty No. 13 lands and territory of the Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation and the \nlands of the Anishinabek of the Williams Treaties First Nations. Our campuses are also situated on the traditional territorie s of the Huron-Wendat \nand the Haudenosaunee. Treaty history is foundational, and it is our collective responsibility to honour the land, as we honour and respect those \nwho have gone before us, those who are here and those who have yet to come. We are grateful for the opportunity to be learnin g, working and \nthriving on this land, and we commit to learn the truth and be active in the process of reconciliation. "
    },
    "https://www.yorku.ca/unit/vpf/wp-content/uploads/sites/1070/2026/09/PDV_FinIn.pdf": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Position in Financial Inclusion  and Inclusive Insurance (Department of",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Position in Financial Inclusion  and Inclusive Insurance (Department of \nEconomics, York University, Toronto, Canada) \nThe Department of Economics at York University invites applications for a Postdoctoral \nVisitor (PDV) position to join an international research program on financial inclusion, \nhousehold decision -making under risk, and inclusive insurance in developing econ omies. \nThe successful candidate will also be appointed as a Scholar with the RISC Foundation in \nToronto and will play an active role in the Foundation’s Consortium for the 17 Goals Agenda, \nwhich brings together researchers from Y ork University, the University of Lausanne in \nSwitzerland, and the University of Liverpool in the United Kingdom. \nThis is a full -time, in -person, one -year appointment based at York University in Toronto  \nunder the supervision of Professor Ida Ferrara , with the possibility of renewal for a second \nyear, subject to funding availability and satisfactory performance. The anticipated start date \nis January 1, 2027. \nAbout the Research Program \nThe successful candidate will join an international partnership involving academic \nresearchers and research organizations in Canada, Ethiopia, Indonesia, Morocco, \nSwitzerland, and the United Kingdom. The program investigates how financial inclusion and \ninclusive insurance can strengthen household resilience to economic, health, and climate \nshocks while contributing to the development of sustainable and inclusive insurance \nmarkets. \nA major component of the program involves the development and analysis of household \nsurvey data across multiple developing-country settings. The research combines large-scale \nempirical analysis with theoretical modelling and addresses topics including: \n• financial inclusion and household vulnerability; \n• insurance demand and household decision-making under risk; \n• financial literacy, trust, attitudes, and risk perceptions; \n• gender and socio-economic inequalities in access to financial protection; \n• survey design and field data collection; and \n• insurance pricing, risk pooling, portfolio diversification, and the sustainability of \ninclusive insurance markets. \nThe program draws on economics, behavioural science, risk management, and quantitative \nmethods and seeks to produce rigorous academic research alongside research with direct \npolicy relevance. \nResponsibilities \nThe PDV will play a central research role in the program, with particular responsibility for \nits empirical components, and will work closely with Professor Ida Ferrara as the Principal \nInvestigator and an international network of collaborators. \nThe successful candidate will: \n• contribute to the design and implementation of household surveys; \n• coordinate pilot studies and data collection activities with research partners in \nEthiopia, Morocco, and Indonesia; \n• manage, clean, and analyze large household survey datasets; \n• conduct advanced econometric analyses; \n• contribute to the development of empirical research designs and research \nmethodologies; \n• lead and contribute to manuscripts for submission to peer-reviewed journals; \n• mentor and work with undergraduate and graduate students and research assistants; \n• contribute to conference presentations, research workshops, and policy outputs; and \n• contribute to the coordination of research activities across partner institutions. \nThe PDV will have opportunities to take intellectual leadership on empirical research arising \nfrom the program and to develop related research within the broader themes of the project. \nInternational travel may be required. \nTeaching Opportunities \nOccasional teaching may be available depending on departmental needs. \nQualifications \nApplicants must have: \n• a PhD in Economics or a closely related discipline completed before the appointment \nbegins; \n• research experience applying microeconomic or quantitative behavioural analysis to \ndevelopment, financial inclusion, labour, health, or risk-related topics; \n• strong econometric and quantitative research skills; \n• experience working with household or other micro-level data; \n• proficiency in statistical software such as Stata, R, Python, or equivalent; \n• excellent written and oral communication skills; and \n• a strong research record and demonstrated potential to publish in high-quality peer-\nreviewed journals. \nThe following qualifications would be considered assets: \n• experience with household survey design and implementation; \n• experience with field experiments or other experimental or quasi -experimental \nresearch designs; \n• experience conducting research in developing economies; \n• knowledge of financial inclusion, insurance, household risk, or poverty and \nvulnerability; \n• experience mentoring or working with graduate students and research assistants; \nand \n• interest in working within an interdisciplinary and international research \nenvironment. \nResearch Environment \nThe PDV will join an active research environment at York University and collaborate with \nresearchers and partner organizations across several countries. The position provides \nopportunities to take a leading role in original empirical research, contribute t o the \ndevelopment of new international household datasets, collaborate with an international \nnetwork of researchers, present research at academic conferences and workshops, and \ncontribute to research with direct relevance for policy and practice. \nThe appointment also provides an opportunity to develop an independent research profile \nwithin the broader themes of financial inclusion, household risk, insurance, and \ndevelopment. \nSalary and Benefits \nThe annual salary range is CAD $57,000–$70,000, commensurate with qualifications and \nexperience. \nThe successful candidate will receive benefits and leaves applicable to Postdoctoral Visitors \nin accordance with York University policies and the applicable collective agreement. \nApplication \nApplicants should submit: \n• a cover letter describing their research interests, relevant experience, and suitability \nfor the position; \n• a curriculum vitae; \n• one representative research paper, which may be published or unpublished; and \n• the names and contact information of three referees. \nApplications should be submitted by email to iferrara@yorku.ca. \nReview of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. \nApplications received by November 15 , 2026 , will receive full consideration.  Only those \napplicants selected for consideration will be contacted for an in-person or Zoom interview. \nThe anticipated start date is January 1, 2027. \nYork University is committed to equity, diversity, and inclusion and welcomes applications \nfrom all qualified candidates. \n "
    }
  },
